window.TFTD_DATA = [{"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-D49cvkrKnVw", "title": "HERE is the Most Important Gift We Can Give | Thought For the Day #87", "short_title": "HERE is the Most Important Gift We Can Give", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 87, "blurb": "This is more valuable than silver and gold...", "date": "2026-07-08", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-D49cvkrKnVw.mp3", "duration": 89, "audio_local": "audio/001-D49cvkrKnVw.mp3", "transcript": "The most important gift that we can give another person is not an item, and it's not a sacrifice. It is to give them what is most alive in us. It's a sharing of what is important to us, what's happening for us: our joy, our interests, our understanding, our knowledge, our humor, even our sadness. All of those things are genuine sharings of ourselves. And when we share of ourselves, it is the most important thing that we can give someone because it is not replaceable. It is a genuine connection with another person. And what that does for another person if they receive it is it brings something alive in them, it makes them feel more alive by experiencing that together with you, by having received that sharing from you. And the reality is that is exactly what God gives us in the very act of creation. The very act of creation is a sharing of what is alive and exciting for him. The whole of creation is essentially that, along with our own lives. And so our experience of that, our interaction with that, is enriching to us, it's enlivening to us, and it is what we can do with each other.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The most important gift that we can give another person is not an item, and it's not a sacrifice."], [7.24, "It is to give them what is most alive in us."], [11.96, "It's a sharing of what is important to us, what's happening for us: our joy, our interests, our understanding, our knowledge, our humor, even our sadness."], [24.66, "All of those things are genuine sharings of ourselves."], [29.64, "And when we share of ourselves, it is the most important thing that we can give someone because it is not replaceable."], [38.02, "It is a genuine connection with another person."], [41.46, "And what that does for another person if they receive it is it brings something alive in them, it makes them feel more alive by experiencing that together with you, by having received that sharing from you."], [54.86, "And the reality is that is exactly what God gives us in the very act of creation."], [62.64, "The very act of creation is a sharing of what is alive and exciting for him."], [67.98, "The whole of creation is essentially that, along with our own lives."], [73.16, "And so our experience of that, our interaction with that, is enriching to us, it's enlivening to us, and it is what we can do with each other."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-v6p2LDVwfgo", "title": "AI & Creativity | Thought For the Day #86", "short_title": "AI & Creativity", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 86, "blurb": "In a world in which AI is becoming more prevalent, what is the place for humans?", "date": "2026-07-07", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-v6p2LDVwfgo.mp3", "duration": 94, "audio_local": "audio/001-v6p2LDVwfgo.mp3", "transcript": "The תורה <i>Torah</i> tells us many times about שבת <i>Shabbat</i>, but what we don't always pay attention to is that more often than not when it presents שבת <i>Shabbat</i> to us, it doesn't present it to us in isolation. It says, ששת ימים תעשה מלאכה וביום השביעי תשבות <i>Sheshet yamim teaseh melacha uveyom hashvii tishbot</i>. For six days, do מלאכה <i>melacha</i>, and what מלאכה <i>melacha</i> is is not just work or labor; מלאכה <i>melacha</i> specifically means creative work. The תורה <i>Torah</i> says to us: rest one day a week, but for the other six, be creative, put into the world new things that are true, that are good, that are beautiful, that are productive. And with the advance of AI at exponential rates, the tasks and jobs that human beings have been accustomed to doing for a very long time are going to be supplanted by robots, machines, and computers. What's left for us to do is to be creative, to share of ourselves in the world, to bring something of value to the world that only we can do, and it doesn't have to be tremendously huge out-of-the-box things, it just has to be that we are giving something to the world because we ourselves are already unique and irreplaceable and unrepeatable. What we bring to the world in terms of something true, something good, something beautiful is something uniquely human that nothing can ever replace.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> tells us many times about שבת <i>Shabbat</i>, but what we don't always pay attention to is that more often than not when it presents שבת <i>Shabbat</i> to us, it doesn't present it to us in isolation."], [11.4, "It says, ששת ימים תעשה מלאכה וביום השביעי תשבות <i>Sheshet yamim teaseh melacha uveyom hashvii tishbot</i>."], [15.94, "For six days, do מלאכה <i>melacha</i>, and what מלאכה <i>melacha</i> is is not just work or labor; מלאכה <i>melacha</i> specifically means creative work."], [20.52, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> says to us: rest one day a week, but for the other six, be creative, put into the world new things that are true, that are good, that are beautiful, that are productive."], [34.58, "And with the advance of AI at exponential rates, the tasks and jobs that human beings have been accustomed to doing for a very long time are going to be supplanted by robots, machines, and computers."], [47.16, "What's left for us to do is to be creative, to share of ourselves in the world, to bring something of value to the world that only we can do, and it doesn't have to be tremendously huge out-of-the-box things, it just has to be that we are giving something to the world because we ourselves are already unique and irreplaceable and unrepeatable."], [68.66, "What we bring to the world in terms of something true, something good, something beautiful is something uniquely human that nothing can ever replace."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-UjT89xzMdF4", "title": "What is the Most PRODUCTIVE Time? | Thought For the Day #85", "short_title": "What is the Most PRODUCTIVE Time?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 85, "blurb": "The power of silence...", "date": "2026-07-05", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-UjT89xzMdF4.mp3", "duration": 80, "audio_local": "audio/001-UjT89xzMdF4.mp3", "transcript": "רבן שמעון בן גמליאל <i>Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel</i> said, \\\"כל ימי גדלתי בין החכמים ולא מצאתי לגוף טוב אלא שתיקה <i>Kol yamai gidalti ben hachachamim velo matzati laguf tov ela shtika</i>.\\\" He said, \\\"I've lived all of my days among the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i>, among the sages, and I found that the best thing for a person is silence.\\\" And one of the reasons for that is because ironically and counter-intuitively, silence is the most productive time, and it is ultimately something that creates the best opportunity for us in life because during times of silence, we have the opportunity, first of all, to listen and to be able to take in what is important for us to know about the world and other ideas that people might have. It also gives us an opportunity to synthesize what it is that we've learned and what it is that we know and to incorporate it. If we are always speaking, active, and on, we don't really have an opportunity to be able to do that. The times of quiet and silence give us an opportunity to be able to absorb and to synthesize in order to be able to incorporate important ideas and important situations into our lives moving forward.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבן שמעון בן גמליאל <i>Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel</i> said, \\\"כל ימי גדלתי בין החכמים ולא מצאתי לגוף טוב אלא שתיקה <i>Kol yamai gidalti ben hachachamim velo matzati laguf tov ela shtika</i>.\\\" He said, \\\"I've lived all of my days among the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i>, among the sages, and I found that the best thing for a person is silence.\\\" And one of the reasons for that is because ironically and counter-intuitively, silence is the most productive time, and it is ultimately something that creates the best opportunity for us in life because during times of silence, we have the opportunity, first of all, to listen and to be able to take in what is important for us to know about the world and other ideas that people might have."], [37.94, "It also gives us an opportunity to synthesize what it is that we've learned and what it is that we know and to incorporate it."], [42.14, "If we are always speaking, active, and on, we don't really have an opportunity to be able to do that."], [47.78, "The times of quiet and silence give us an opportunity to be able to absorb and to synthesize in order to be able to incorporate important ideas and important situations into our lives moving forward."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-WqfC3pLjYzI", "title": "Logic and Meaning | Thought For the Day #84", "short_title": "Logic and Meaning", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 84, "blurb": "There are things that logic and reason cannot approach...", "date": "2026-07-03", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-WqfC3pLjYzI.mp3", "duration": 82, "audio_local": "audio/001-WqfC3pLjYzI.mp3", "transcript": "One issue that we tend to get bogged down with in the West is the obsession with logic. Now, let me be clear, logic and reason is absolutely important and we really do need it in order to be able to settle ourselves in reality. But there are things that logic and reason cannot approach: experiential realities, truths about the world that we can't quite put into words and therefore cannot quite qualify and therefore cannot quite define and square away as we might be able to with airtight logical arguments. And those things still matter. It is usually the case that the highest levels of meaning are the kinds of things that we can't quite explain logically and in succinct terms, but rather things that we experience, that move us to tears, that overwhelm us in their greatness and magnitude. And those things are perhaps the highest levels of meaning and it is important not to belittle them because we can't explain them, but to recognize the magnitude of their presence and to honor the recognition of that meaning.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One issue that we tend to get bogged down with in the West is the obsession with logic."], [5.78, "Now, let me be clear, logic and reason is absolutely important and we really do need it in order to be able to settle ourselves in reality."], [14.58, "But there are things that logic and reason cannot approach: experiential realities, truths about the world that we can't quite put into words and therefore cannot quite qualify and therefore cannot quite define and square away as we might be able to with airtight logical arguments."], [37.88, "And those things still matter."], [41.08, "It is usually the case that the highest levels of meaning are the kinds of things that we can't quite explain logically and in succinct terms, but rather things that we experience, that move us to tears, that overwhelm us in their greatness and magnitude."], [58.86, "And those things are perhaps the highest levels of meaning and it is important not to belittle them because we can't explain them, but to recognize the magnitude of their presence and to honor the recognition of that meaning."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-YS9oPmi6FR4", "title": "99% of THIS is Plagiarized | Thought For the Day #83", "short_title": "99% of THIS is Plagiarized", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 83, "blurb": "All ideas are secondhand...", "date": "2026-07-02", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-YS9oPmi6FR4.mp3", "duration": 104, "audio_local": "audio/001-YS9oPmi6FR4.mp3", "transcript": "In a letter that Mark Twain wrote to Helen Keller, he makes a really interesting point. He says all ideas are secondhand. 99 percent of what human beings put out into the world is plagiarism. And what he essentially meant by that is that we really shouldn't get all up in arms when we hear or read about plagiarism, except for when it is not adjusted, made your own. But otherwise, the reality is, you are hard-pressed to find a brand new idea. This is what שלמה המלך <i>Shlomo Hamelech</i> said way before Mark Twain, when he wrote in קהלת <i>Kohelet</i>, אין כל חדש תחת השמש <i>Ein kol chadash tachat hashemesh</i>. There's nothing new under the sun. But the reality is that pretty much everything that we have in the world is ten centuries of production before us and ten thousand people before us and we're just kind of stepping on top of that and using it in a way that might look a little bit different and a little bit novel. It tells us two things: number one, that the bit of difference that is there, that we bring to it, that's uniquely ours, is really significant, even though 99 percent of it is repackaged, rehashed, and reconfigured from what has been in the world for generations.", "sentences": [[0.0, "In a letter that Mark Twain wrote to Helen Keller, he makes a really interesting point."], [5.38, "He says all ideas are secondhand."], [7.98, "99 percent of what human beings put out into the world is plagiarism."], [14.74, "And what he essentially meant by that is that we really shouldn't get all up in arms when we hear or read about plagiarism, except for when it is not adjusted, made your own."], [26.72, "But otherwise, the reality is, you are hard-pressed to find a brand new idea."], [32.98, "This is what שלמה המלך <i>Shlomo Hamelech</i> said way before Mark Twain, when he wrote in קהלת <i>Kohelet</i>, אין כל חדש תחת השמש <i>Ein kol chadash tachat hashemesh</i>."], [40.52, "There's nothing new under the sun."], [43.04, "But the reality is that pretty much everything that we have in the world is ten centuries of production before us and ten thousand people before us and we're just kind of stepping on top of that and using it in a way that might look a little bit different and a little bit novel."], [60.22, "It tells us two things: number one, that the bit of difference that is there, that we bring to it, that's uniquely ours, is really significant, even though 99 percent of it is repackaged, rehashed, and reconfigured from what has been in the world for generations."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-AFG0rYilUns", "title": "HERE is the Most Important Gift We Can Give | Thought For the Day #82", "short_title": "HERE is the Most Important Gift We Can Give", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 82, "blurb": "The most important gift we can give others isn’t an item or a sacrifice, but the sharing of what is most alive in us—our joy, interests, understanding, knowledge, humor, and even sadness...", "date": "2026-06-29", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-AFG0rYilUns.mp3", "duration": 85, "audio_local": "audio/001-AFG0rYilUns.mp3", "transcript": "The most important gift that we can give another person is not an item, and it's not a sacrifice. It is to give them what is most alive in us. It's a sharing of what is important to us, what's happening for us: our joy, our interests, our understanding, our knowledge, our humor, even our sadness. All of those things are genuine sharings of ourselves. And when we share of ourselves, it is the most important thing that we can give someone because it is not replaceable. It is a genuine connection with another person. And what that does for another person if they receive it, it brings something alive in them, it makes them feel more alive by experiencing that together with you, by having received that sharing from you. And the reality is that that is exactly what God gives us in the very act of creation. The very act of creation is a sharing of what is alive and exciting for him. The whole of creation is essentially that, along with our own lives. And so our experience of that, our interaction with that, is enriching to us, it's enlivening to us, and it is what we can do with each other.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The most important gift that we can give another person is not an item, and it's not a sacrifice."], [7.42, "It is to give them what is most alive in us."], [11.96, "It's a sharing of what is important to us, what's happening for us: our joy, our interests, our understanding, our knowledge, our humor, even our sadness."], [24.6, "All of those things are genuine sharings of ourselves."], [29.6, "And when we share of ourselves, it is the most important thing that we can give someone because it is not replaceable."], [37.9, "It is a genuine connection with another person."], [41.44, "And what that does for another person if they receive it, it brings something alive in them, it makes them feel more alive by experiencing that together with you, by having received that sharing from you."], [54.08, "And the reality is that that is exactly what God gives us in the very act of creation."], [61.86, "The very act of creation is a sharing of what is alive and exciting for him."], [67.14, "The whole of creation is essentially that, along with our own lives."], [72.34, "And so our experience of that, our interaction with that, is enriching to us, it's enlivening to us, and it is what we can do with each other."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-UQHpRjP33pg", "title": "WANT to Want | Thought For the Day #81", "short_title": "WANT to Want", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 81, "blurb": "It's important to make a distinction between wanting to do something and wanting to want to do something.", "date": "2026-06-26", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-UQHpRjP33pg.mp3", "duration": 103, "audio_local": "audio/001-UQHpRjP33pg.mp3", "transcript": "It's important to make a distinction between wanting to do something and wanting to want to do something. They are not the same. To want to do something means that you actually do want to do the thing, and usually when that is the case, you accomplish it. Although there may be obstacles to that, when that incentive of genuine want is there, it is more likely than not that you will ultimately accomplish whatever it is that you want to genuinely accomplish. But there is also the wanting to want to do something, and that is different. It is the recognition that there is something that you would like to do, but you don't really have the drive necessary or the incentive necessary in order to actually do it because there are, in reality, things that you want more other than it. And so while you recognize it as being valuable, you want to want to do it, but you don't want to do it because in reality you want to do something else more. And in our lives we sometimes get frustrated because we tell ourselves that we want to do something, when in reality we don't quite want to, we want to want to, and there's an extra step that we have to make in terms of moving from wanting to want to to actually wanting to, and then doing it.", "sentences": [[0.52, "It's important to make a distinction between wanting to do something and wanting to want to do something."], [8.88, "They are not the same."], [10.64, "To want to do something means that you actually do want to do the thing, and usually when that is the case, you accomplish it."], [20.02, "Although there may be obstacles to that, when that incentive of genuine want is there, it is more likely than not that you will ultimately accomplish whatever it is that you want to genuinely accomplish."], [34.8, "But there is also the wanting to want to do something, and that is different."], [40.92, "It is the recognition that there is something that you would like to do, but you don't really have the drive necessary or the incentive necessary in order to actually do it because there are, in reality, things that you want more other than it."], [60.32, "And so while you recognize it as being valuable, you want to want to do it, but you don't want to do it because in reality you want to do something else more."], [74.04, "And in our lives we sometimes get frustrated because we tell ourselves that we want to do something, when in reality we don't quite want to, we want to want to, and there's an extra step that we have to make in terms of moving from wanting to want to to actually wanting to, and then doing it."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-rlALGwZz-g8", "title": "POSITION Yourself Well | Thought For the Day #80", "short_title": "POSITION Yourself Well", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 80, "blurb": "All options improve when our positioning is strong, and Torah is valuable because it aims to position us well so we can handle life as it continually comes...", "date": "2026-06-25", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-rlALGwZz-g8.mp3", "duration": 64, "audio_local": "audio/001-rlALGwZz-g8.mp3", "transcript": "All options are better when our positioning is strong. It's one of the reasons why תורה <i>Torah</i> practically is so valuable and helpful to us. תורה <i>Torah</i> aims to position us well in order that when life comes to us, which is of course all the time, we are well positioned to be able to deal with it. So the מצוות <i>Mitzvot</i> of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the teachings of the תורה <i>Torah</i> encourage us to position ourselves well, to have good positions in terms of our health, to have good positions in terms of our relationships, to have good positions in terms of our finances, to have good positions in terms of our own development. All of those things create better options for us than we would otherwise have if any of those things or all of them were in poorer position. It has direct implications on the options that are open for us in life and our ability to be able to move ahead successfully in the lives that we live.", "sentences": [[0.0, "All options are better when our positioning is strong."], [4.48, "It's one of the reasons why תורה <i>Torah</i> practically is so valuable and helpful to us."], [10.46, "תורה <i>Torah</i> aims to position us well in order that when life comes to us, which is of course all the time, we are well positioned to be able to deal with it."], [20.06, "So the מצוות <i>Mitzvot</i> of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the teachings of the תורה <i>Torah</i> encourage us to position ourselves well, to have good positions in terms of our health, to have good positions in terms of our relationships, to have good positions in terms of our finances, to have good positions in terms of our own development."], [37.26, "All of those things create better options for us than we would otherwise have if any of those things or all of them were in poorer position."], [46.88, "It has direct implications on the options that are open for us in life and our ability to be able to move ahead successfully in the lives that we live."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-Rgua3Luqw9Y", "title": "Err on the Side of MERCY | Thought For the Day #79", "short_title": "Err on the Side of MERCY", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 79, "blurb": "When in doubt, mery wins...", "date": "2026-06-12", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-Rgua3Luqw9Y.mp3", "duration": 50, "audio_local": "audio/001-Rgua3Luqw9Y.mp3", "transcript": "רבי אברהם בן הרמב\\\"ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> speaking further on mercy says, however, even though there are situations in which mercy should be checked and you shouldn't be kind and caring to people when you know that they are going to be regularly counterproductive and hurt both themselves and others. Nonetheless, he says, when you're not sure whether mercy should be had here or not, better to err on the side of mercy. Better to be merciful rather than not merciful out of doubt, because that ultimately is an attribute of kindness and it's something that is good for us in our own behaviors to learn how to be warm and open rather than cold and callous.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבי אברהם בן הרמב\\\"ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> speaking further on mercy says, however, even though there are situations in which mercy should be checked and you shouldn't be kind and caring to people when you know that they are going to be regularly counterproductive and hurt both themselves and others."], [17.86, "Nonetheless, he says, when you're not sure whether mercy should be had here or not, better to err on the side of mercy."], [26.5, "Better to be merciful rather than not merciful out of doubt, because that ultimately is an attribute of kindness and it's something that is good for us in our own behaviors to learn how to be warm and open rather than cold and callous."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-DCKVNqHBl-s", "title": "The SHOCKING Truth About Mercy | Thought For the Day #78", "short_title": "The SHOCKING Truth About Mercy", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 78, "blurb": "Constant compassion can sometimes break things down when it enables people who seek to undermine or destroy...", "date": "2026-06-11", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-DCKVNqHBl-s.mp3", "duration": 88, "audio_local": "audio/001-DCKVNqHBl-s.mp3", "transcript": "Rabbi אברהם <i>Avraham</i> בן <i>ben</i> הרמב״ם <i>HaRambam</i> writes that mercy is complex because although we would think that being merciful all the time is a good thing, sometimes it serves to break things down if people are not really fit to receive mercy because they would wish to destroy things or because they would wish to undermine things and we just keep having mercy upon them, that's not really mercy. Instead, it's enabling. We enable people to perpetuate and continue in their poor behaviors and therefore sometimes it's inappropriate to be merciful to people, to do kindness for people. So for example if a person is continuously engaging in bad behaviors or even in self-destructive behaviors, to keep giving them leeway, to support them in various ways with money, with accommodation and so on, may very well be a counterproductive thing to do. And so we have to be very careful in deciding how it is that we give mercy and kindness and care to people when they are committed to doing things that are counterproductive both to themselves and to others.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Rabbi אברהם <i>Avraham</i> בן <i>ben</i> הרמב״ם <i>HaRambam</i> writes that mercy is complex because although we would think that being merciful all the time is a good thing, sometimes it serves to break things down if people are not really fit to receive mercy because they would wish to destroy things or because they would wish to undermine things and we just keep having mercy upon them, that's not really mercy."], [24.0, "Instead, it's enabling."], [26.0, "We enable people to perpetuate and continue in their poor behaviors and therefore sometimes it's inappropriate to be merciful to people, to do kindness for people."], [37.06, "So for example if a person is continuously engaging in bad behaviors or even in self-destructive behaviors, to keep giving them leeway, to support them in various ways with money, with accommodation and so on, may very well be a counterproductive thing to do."], [55.38, "And so we have to be very careful in deciding how it is that we give mercy and kindness and care to people when they are committed to doing things that are counterproductive both to themselves and to others."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-mbxCLRvXg44", "title": "Learn It AGAIN | Thought For the Day #77", "short_title": "Learn It AGAIN", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 77, "blurb": "Even if you learned something when you were younger, learn it again with a fresh perspective when you are older.\n\nInsight from Hovot HaLebabot.", "date": "2026-06-09", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-mbxCLRvXg44.mp3", "duration": 92, "audio_local": "audio/001-mbxCLRvXg44.mp3", "transcript": "רבנו בחיי אבן פקודה <i>Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Paquda</i> writes in the חובות הלבבות <i>Chovot haLevavot</i> that one must consider what one has learned in their younger days. What you learned as a child remains with you. In a child's mind, whatever it is that you learn in your early life stays that way in your mind. And so, he says that things that we have learned when we were younger are important to revisit as we get older because we can then look at them again and understand them in a more mature frame with a more mature mindset. And so, especially the things in תורה <i>Torah</i> that we learned when we were younger or children, we really must revisit them in adulthood and as we get older because every year that we get older we understand things differently. And so, one of the things that is valuable in terms of תורה <i>Torah</i> is that we read the whole תורה <i>Torah</i> every year in a cycle. And when we read the תורה <i>Torah</i> in our twenties, it's not like reading תורה <i>Torah</i> in our forties or fifties or beyond. We get older, we understand the world differently, and we then see things in what we learned from that perspective that could never have come out to us in the younger years. Always good to revisit our learning when we get older to be able to understand it in a more mature way.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבנו בחיי אבן פקודה <i>Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Paquda</i> writes in the חובות הלבבות <i>Chovot haLevavot</i> that one must consider what one has learned in their younger days."], [10.82, "What you learned as a child remains with you."], [13.14, "In a child's mind, whatever it is that you learn in your early life stays that way in your mind."], [20.96, "And so, he says that things that we have learned when we were younger are important to revisit as we get older because we can then look at them again and understand them in a more mature frame with a more mature mindset."], [35.92, "And so, especially the things in תורה <i>Torah</i> that we learned when we were younger or children, we really must revisit them in adulthood and as we get older because every year that we get older we understand things differently."], [49.76, "And so, one of the things that is valuable in terms of תורה <i>Torah</i> is that we read the whole תורה <i>Torah</i> every year in a cycle."], [56.7, "And when we read the תורה <i>Torah</i> in our twenties, it's not like reading תורה <i>Torah</i> in our forties or fifties or beyond."], [63.06, "We get older, we understand the world differently, and we then see things in what we learned from that perspective that could never have come out to us in the younger years."], [73.44, "Always good to revisit our learning when we get older to be able to understand it in a more mature way."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-HDo_3vRr7uw", "title": "Share What You KNOW | Thought For the Day #76", "short_title": "Share What You KNOW", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 76, "blurb": "Be generous in sharing the things you have learned...\n\nInsight from Rabbi Abraham ben HaRambam.", "date": "2026-06-08", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-HDo_3vRr7uw.mp3", "duration": 61, "audio_local": "audio/001-HDo_3vRr7uw.mp3", "transcript": "The third kind of generosity that רבי אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> speaks about is the generosity of wisdom. That this world is a world that is better when we understand it, when we can come to know what it is that's going on and reduce uncertainty and confusion in that. But wisdom is not always easy to come by. And if you are a person that has wisdom, that has spent time learning and understanding, to give of that to someone for no ulterior motives, not because they have a claim on you, not because they pay you, simply because you're generously offering it and giving it to someone, that too is walking in God's ways. It's something that is similar to the ways of God. And when we can be generous that way, when we can teach people, when we can offer knowledge and wisdom to people, it is a great form of generosity that helps the world and makes things better.", "sentences": [[0.72, "The third kind of generosity that רבי אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> speaks about is the generosity of wisdom."], [8.62, "That this world is a world that is better when we understand it, when we can come to know what it is that's going on and reduce uncertainty and confusion in that."], [18.72, "But wisdom is not always easy to come by."], [21.5, "And if you are a person that has wisdom, that has spent time learning and understanding, to give of that to someone for no ulterior motives, not because they have a claim on you, not because they pay you, simply because you're generously offering it and giving it to someone, that too is walking in God's ways."], [40.98, "It's something that is similar to the ways of God."], [43.76, "And when we can be generous that way, when we can teach people, when we can offer knowledge and wisdom to people, it is a great form of generosity that helps the world and makes things better."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-0qAmk9ff8lM", "title": "One EASY Thing You Can Do Right Now | Pearls From Pirke Abot #19", "short_title": "One EASY Thing You Can Do Right Now", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 19, "blurb": "Here's some ancient wisdom for our modern lives.", "date": "2026-06-06", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-0qAmk9ff8lM.mp3", "duration": 69, "audio_local": "audio/001-0qAmk9ff8lM.mp3", "transcript": "The second type of generosity that רבי אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> outlines is honor, and he says that what a person can do if they have honor, if people honor them and recognize them as people, they can use that honor and bestow it to others in the form of endorsement. And we all recognize in humanity that endorsement is really, really important for us, probably more important than we actually consciously are aware. But when we actually hold people in high regard, we trust people because of the high regard that we hold them, and they say to us, this individual is somebody worth reading, this individual is somebody that is worth paying attention to, we take that very seriously. And that kind of giving of honor from somebody who holds honor itself is a form of generosity because as God has his great honor, he has given honor to and dignity to his creations, and that's something that we can do in our lives as well.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The second type of generosity that רבי אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam</i> outlines is honor, and he says that what a person can do if they have honor, if people honor them and recognize them as people, they can use that honor and bestow it to others in the form of endorsement."], [22.34, "And we all recognize in humanity that endorsement is really, really important for us, probably more important than we actually consciously are aware."], [31.22, "But when we actually hold people in high regard, we trust people because of the high regard that we hold them, and they say to us, this individual is somebody worth reading, this individual is somebody that is worth paying attention to, we take that very seriously."], [46.26, "And that kind of giving of honor from somebody who holds honor itself is a form of generosity because as God has his great honor, he has given honor to and dignity to his creations, and that's something that we can do in our lives as well."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-vg_RemiuKsk", "title": "What is GENEROSITY? | Pearls From Pirke Abot #18", "short_title": "What is GENEROSITY?", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 18, "blurb": "What are the steps you can take to be generous?", "date": "2026-06-05", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-vg_RemiuKsk.mp3", "duration": 82, "audio_local": "audio/001-vg_RemiuKsk.mp3", "transcript": "What is generosity? The basic definition of generosity is giving someone something that you have that you can spare that they don't really have a claim on. You're not in debt to them, they're not expecting it from you. רב אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Reb Avraham ben HaRambam</i> gives three examples of generosity. And in the next three videos, I'll talk about each one of those examples. The first is fairly obvious and that is of course with money. If you have money that you can give to another person or if you have assets that you can supply another person with that would make their life better, that would help them and you deem it to be something that is productive, well that is a wonderful attribute. It's called generosity, נדיבות <i>Nedivut</i>. And that is something that actually connects us to God because it is one of God's attributes. God gives what He does not need to give. One of the most important things, life itself, creation itself is a generous act of God that He does not need to give nor do we demand of Him. But there it is. So when we can give financial support to people in a situation in which they don't have a claim against us and we don't have to do it, it is an element of walking in God's ways. It is being generous as He is generous.", "sentences": [[0.64, "What is generosity?"], [2.5, "The basic definition of generosity is giving someone something that you have that you can spare that they don't really have a claim on."], [11.06, "You're not in debt to them, they're not expecting it from you."], [14.04, "רב אברהם בן הרמב״ם <i>Reb Avraham ben HaRambam</i> gives three examples of generosity."], [19.24, "And in the next three videos, I'll talk about each one of those examples."], [22.76, "The first is fairly obvious and that is of course with money."], [27.0, "If you have money that you can give to another person or if you have assets that you can supply another person with that would make their life better, that would help them and you deem it to be something that is productive, well that is a wonderful attribute."], [40.58, "It's called generosity, נדיבות <i>Nedivut</i>."], [43.4, "And that is something that actually connects us to God because it is one of God's attributes."], [48.9, "God gives what He does not need to give."], [51.36, "One of the most important things, life itself, creation itself is a generous act of God that He does not need to give nor do we demand of Him."], [61.5, "But there it is."], [62.7, "So when we can give financial support to people in a situation in which they don't have a claim against us and we don't have to do it, it is an element of walking in God's ways."], [73.66, "It is being generous as He is generous."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-sNLWkpFY1hU", "title": "Unmasking TRUTH | Pearls From Pirke Abot #17", "short_title": "Unmasking TRUTH", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 17, "blurb": "One of the most important ways that we serve God is with being honest with ourselves, truthful with ourselves.", "date": "2026-06-04", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-sNLWkpFY1hU.mp3", "duration": 54, "audio_local": "audio/001-sNLWkpFY1hU.mp3", "transcript": "One of the most important ways that we serve God is with being honest with ourselves, truthful with ourselves. The connection that we have with God essentially is in truth as the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say חותמו של הקדוש ברוך הוא אמת <i>Chotamo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu Emet</i>, the very seal of God is truth. And so the element of lies that are within us keep us from God. They essentially act as barriers. And one of the most important things that we can come to truth on is about ourselves. What about us is working from ego? What about us is working from ulterior motives? When we can be courageous enough and brave enough to regularly examine what it is that's happening in us that we might not be truthful about helps us to be able to build our relationship with God and invite Him into our lives.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the most important ways that we serve God is with being honest with ourselves, truthful with ourselves."], [7.56, "The connection that we have with God essentially is in truth as the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say חותמו של הקדוש ברוך הוא אמת <i>Chotamo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu Emet</i>, the very seal of God is truth."], [14.6, "And so the element of lies that are within us keep us from God."], [18.5, "They essentially act as barriers."], [20.62, "And one of the most important things that we can come to truth on is about ourselves."], [26.42, "What about us is working from ego?"], [29.54, "What about us is working from ulterior motives?"], [32.88, "When we can be courageous enough and brave enough to regularly examine what it is that's happening in us that we might not be truthful about helps us to be able to build our relationship with God and invite Him into our lives."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-KondtowwbOo", "title": "The HONOR Dilemma | Pearls From Pirke Abot #17", "short_title": "The HONOR Dilemma", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 17, "blurb": "What is the Jewish attitude to honor?", "date": "2026-05-30", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-KondtowwbOo.mp3", "duration": 74, "audio_local": "audio/001-KondtowwbOo.mp3", "transcript": "The third thing that pulls us out of the world is כבוד <i>Kavod</i>, honor. And what that's saying is when we look for honor or when we seek to be honored by others, we are distracting from our own natural weight of being. Because there is no real honor that one could actually give us in the sense of giving us something that we don't already have. Our honor is our own existence. It is the very soul that God breathed into each and every single one of us. There is no one else that can give us that honor aside from God and our own personal awareness and recognition of it. So when we look for it to be filled by others, when we look for ourselves to be validated by others, made okay by others, it pulls us out of the world because it distracts and disregards the very real beautiful self that is given to us by God that is in our own hands from the day that we are born. And when we keep looking for it from the outside, we do not pay attention to the true honor that always exists within us.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The third thing that pulls us out of the world is כבוד <i>Kavod</i>, honor."], [4.02, "And what that's saying is when we look for honor or when we seek to be honored by others, we are distracting from our own natural weight of being."], [16.7, "Because there is no real honor that one could actually give us in the sense of giving us something that we don't already have."], [24.26, "Our honor is our own existence."], [27.54, "It is the very soul that God breathed into each and every single one of us."], [32.56, "There is no one else that can give us that honor aside from God and our own personal awareness and recognition of it."], [38.84, "So when we look for it to be filled by others, when we look for ourselves to be validated by others, made okay by others, it pulls us out of the world because it distracts and disregards the very real beautiful self that is given to us by God that is in our own hands from the day that we are born."], [59.76, "And when we keep looking for it from the outside, we do not pay attention to the true honor that always exists within us."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-8DZYJBe93Yo", "title": "The Trap of DESIRE | Pearls From Pirke Abot #16", "short_title": "The Trap of DESIRE", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 16, "blurb": "Desire pulls us out of the world...", "date": "2026-05-29", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/002-8DZYJBe93Yo.mp3", "duration": 63, "audio_local": "audio/002-8DZYJBe93Yo.mp3", "transcript": "The second thing that pulls a person out of the world according to the משנה <i>Mishnah</i> is תאוה <i>Ta'avah</i>, is desire. And what that means is that when we allow for our desires to overtake us in the physical sense, when our senses are driving us and what it is we want to eat and feel and experience takes over our life, that pulls us out of the world because they don't really come from the very nature of who we are as individuals. They come from genetic drives that are really only geared for survival and reproduction. And when we give in to those things, we erase our unique individual selves and our own highest selves in favor of those drives being met. And when we do that, we pull ourselves out of the world because we are essentially saying, my highest self is not going to be my aim. I'm going to feed the drives that use me as an individual just to be able to get ahead and survive.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The second thing that pulls a person out of the world according to the משנה <i>Mishnah</i> is תאוה <i>Ta'avah</i>, is desire."], [6.18, "And what that means is that when we allow for our desires to overtake us in the physical sense, when our senses are driving us and what it is we want to eat and feel and experience takes over our life, that pulls us out of the world because they don't really come from the very nature of who we are as individuals."], [27.34, "They come from genetic drives that are really only geared for survival and reproduction."], [32.64, "And when we give in to those things, we erase our unique individual selves and our own highest selves in favor of those drives being met."], [44.26, "And when we do that, we pull ourselves out of the world because we are essentially saying, my highest self is not going to be my aim."], [51.14, "I'm going to feed the drives that use me as an individual just to be able to get ahead and survive."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-6Mcox3qe_38", "title": "AVOID Jealousy | Pearls From Pirke Abot #15", "short_title": "AVOID Jealousy", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 15, "blurb": "Jealousy can destroy us...", "date": "2026-05-28", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/003-6Mcox3qe_38.mp3", "duration": 63, "audio_local": "audio/003-6Mcox3qe_38.mp3", "transcript": "The משנה <i>Mishna</i> says there are three things that pull a person out of the world. They are קנאה <i>Kinah</i>, תאווה <i>Ta'avah</i>, and כבוד <i>Kavod</i>: jealousy, desire, and honor. And those three things we will look at over the next three days. The first is jealousy. Jealousy pulls a person out of the world because it completely distracts an individual from their own life. When a person is jealous they are essentially looking at another person's life and thinking that life should be mine, what that person has is what I should have. And the focus on another person's life and what another person has completely distracts a person, an individual, from their own life. And in doing so, it pulls them out of the world, it pulls their very existence and individual presence out of the world because they're not really living their lives in their own terms, they're living their lives focused on what someone else has or does.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The משנה <i>Mishna</i> says there are three things that pull a person out of the world."], [6.14, "They are קנאה <i>Kinah</i>, תאווה <i>Ta'avah</i>, and כבוד <i>Kavod</i>: jealousy, desire, and honor."], [10.72, "And those three things we will look at over the next three days."], [14.54, "The first is jealousy."], [16.7, "Jealousy pulls a person out of the world because it completely distracts an individual from their own life."], [23.46, "When a person is jealous they are essentially looking at another person's life and thinking that life should be mine, what that person has is what I should have."], [32.48, "And the focus on another person's life and what another person has completely distracts a person, an individual, from their own life."], [41.4, "And in doing so, it pulls them out of the world, it pulls their very existence and individual presence out of the world because they're not really living their lives in their own terms, they're living their lives focused on what someone else has or does."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-zY6gKPIc3OE", "title": "The Power of CLARITY | Pearls From Pirke Abot #14", "short_title": "The Power of CLARITY", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 14, "blurb": "Teshuba can be the key to unlock great heights...", "date": "2026-05-26", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/004-zY6gKPIc3OE.mp3", "duration": 54, "audio_local": "audio/004-zY6gKPIc3OE.mp3", "transcript": "Rabbi Yaakov says something remarkable about תשובה <i>teshuva</i> and עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i>. He says יפה שעה אחת בתשובה בעולם הזה מכל חיי עולם הבא <i>yafa sha'a achas bi'teshuva ba-olam hazeh mikol chayei olam ha-ba</i>. That one hour of תשובה <i>teshuva</i>, of truth, of clarity, of integrity in this world, ומעשים טובים <i>u-ma'asim tovim</i> and the good deeds that come from that place of clarity is greater not just than your life in עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i>, but כל חיי עולם הבא <i>kol chayei olam ha-ba</i>, all of the lives of עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i> put together. And that is essentially saying that the ability to actually do good in this world, to live good and experience the clarity of what is true in this world, it belongs to you in a way that all the lives of עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i> do not reach.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Rabbi Yaakov says something remarkable about תשובה <i>teshuva</i> and עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i>."], [4.5, "He says יפה שעה אחת בתשובה בעולם הזה מכל חיי עולם הבא <i>yafa sha'a achas bi'teshuva ba-olam hazeh mikol chayei olam ha-ba</i>."], [9.62, "That one hour of תשובה <i>teshuva</i>, of truth, of clarity, of integrity in this world, ומעשים טובים <i>u-ma'asim tovim</i> and the good deeds that come from that place of clarity is greater not just than your life in עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i>, but כל חיי עולם הבא <i>kol chayei olam ha-ba</i>, all of the lives of עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i> put together."], [23.32, "And that is essentially saying that the ability to actually do good in this world, to live good and experience the clarity of what is true in this world, it belongs to you in a way that all the lives of עולם הבא <i>olam ha-ba</i> do not reach."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-sVQ2oMrzhuk", "title": "True POWER | Pearls From Pirke Abot #13", "short_title": "True POWER", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 13, "blurb": "What does it mean to be truly powerful?", "date": "2026-05-25", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/005-sVQ2oMrzhuk.mp3", "duration": 59, "audio_local": "audio/005-sVQ2oMrzhuk.mp3", "transcript": "Ben Zoma says, איזהו גיבור הכובש את יצרו <i>Eizehu gibor hakovesh et yitzro</i>. Who's really considered powerful? A powerful person is not someone who is able to best someone in a physical fight. A powerful person is not somebody who uses their control or their command in order to be able to subdue others. All of that is focused on others. The most powerful individual is one who can control their own self, who has the ability to be able to manage their own life and disciplines. It is amongst the most difficult things we do, and all too often we look to see how it is we can control others, how it is that we can use our power to be able to manipulate others, but ultimately, the greatest power comes in the ability to be able to control ourselves for our greatest good.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Ben Zoma says, איזהו גיבור הכובש את יצרו <i>Eizehu gibor hakovesh et yitzro</i>."], [3.46, "Who's really considered powerful?"], [6.0, "A powerful person is not someone who is able to best someone in a physical fight."], [11.8, "A powerful person is not somebody who uses their control or their command in order to be able to subdue others."], [18.96, "All of that is focused on others."], [21.48, "The most powerful individual is one who can control their own self, who has the ability to be able to manage their own life and disciplines."], [34.06, "It is amongst the most difficult things we do, and all too often we look to see how it is we can control others, how it is that we can use our power to be able to manipulate others, but ultimately, the greatest power comes in the ability to be able to control ourselves for our greatest good."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-k3J0Yw0w0cg", "title": "Torah is a FRAMEWORK | Pearls From Pirke Abot #12", "short_title": "Torah is a FRAMEWORK", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 12, "blurb": "The Torah is a vessel. What does that mean?", "date": "2026-05-21", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-k3J0Yw0w0cg.mp3", "duration": 78, "audio_local": "audio/001-k3J0Yw0w0cg.mp3", "transcript": "The תורה <i>Torah</i> is called a כלי חמדה <i>Keli Chemda</i>, which means that it is a delightful desirable vessel. But it's interesting that the תורה <i>Torah</i> is referred to as a vessel. And one of the ways to understand this is that the תורה <i>Torah</i> is not just a book of information, it's not just a book of stories, it's not just a book of law. It is a framework for living. It provides for us a lens through which we're able to see our own lives and the world around us and how it is that we're supposed to value the things that matter. And in that regard, it's easier to understand why the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> put such emphasis on the study of תורה <i>Torah</i> because in studying the תורה <i>Torah</i> we're not just amassing information, we're not just becoming learned and well-read about our history and our stories, we are espousing the very frame of life that has led the Jewish people through history and in a very real and serious way helped the Jewish people not only to survive throughout history with the strongest persecutions that mankind has ever known but also to thrive in spite of it.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> is called a כלי חמדה <i>Keli Chemda</i>, which means that it is a delightful desirable vessel."], [6.18, "But it's interesting that the תורה <i>Torah</i> is referred to as a vessel."], [9.7, "And one of the ways to understand this is that the תורה <i>Torah</i> is not just a book of information, it's not just a book of stories, it's not just a book of law."], [19.2, "It is a framework for living."], [22.64, "It provides for us a lens through which we're able to see our own lives and the world around us and how it is that we're supposed to value the things that matter."], [32.68, "And in that regard, it's easier to understand why the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> put such emphasis on the study of תורה <i>Torah</i> because in studying the תורה <i>Torah</i> we're not just amassing information, we're not just becoming learned and well-read about our history and our stories, we are espousing the very frame of life that has led the Jewish people through history and in a very real and serious way helped the Jewish people not only to survive throughout history with the strongest persecutions that mankind has ever known but also to thrive in spite of it."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-swckDgIcxyw", "title": "Don't Use Torah | Pearls From Pirke Abot #10", "short_title": "Don't Use Torah", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 10, "blurb": "Torah should never be used for personal purposes...", "date": "2026-05-20", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-swckDgIcxyw.mp3", "duration": 68, "audio_local": "audio/001-swckDgIcxyw.mp3", "transcript": "רבי <i>Rabbi</i> צדוק <i>Zadok</i> says something really important about תורה <i>Torah</i>. He says תורה <i>Torah</i> is so precious and important because of the truth that it presents to our lives and the essential framework that it presents to our lives is that it should never be treated transactionally, meaning that a תורה <i>Torah</i> should never be used for personal purposes. אל תעשם עטרה להתגדל בה <i>Al ta'asem atarah l'itgadel bah</i>. Don't use it as a crown in order to be able to gain respect. Never use the knowledge of תורה <i>Torah</i> in order to be able to empower yourself to get ahead. ולא קרדום לחפור בהם <i>V'lo kardom lachpor bahem</i>. Don't use it as a shovel in order to be able to dig with, which essentially means don't build things with the תורה <i>Torah</i> that are beneficial to you. That means that we have to be very careful about the material benefits that we might get from תורה <i>Torah</i> that are transactional in nature. תורה <i>Torah</i> is the framework for life. It is what brings us to our highest selves and the highest values. They are the very ideals that embolden the soul and connect us with God.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבי <i>Rabbi</i> צדוק <i>Zadok</i> says something really important about תורה <i>Torah</i>."], [2.68, "He says תורה <i>Torah</i> is so precious and important because of the truth that it presents to our lives and the essential framework that it presents to our lives is that it should never be treated transactionally, meaning that a תורה <i>Torah</i> should never be used for personal purposes."], [21.98, "אל תעשם עטרה להתגדל בה <i>Al ta'asem atarah l'itgadel bah</i>."], [23.12, "Don't use it as a crown in order to be able to gain respect."], [25.96, "Never use the knowledge of תורה <i>Torah</i> in order to be able to empower yourself to get ahead."], [30.2, "ולא קרדום לחפור בהם <i>V'lo kardom lachpor bahem</i>."], [30.72, "Don't use it as a shovel in order to be able to dig with, which essentially means don't build things with the תורה <i>Torah</i> that are beneficial to you."], [38.08, "That means that we have to be very careful about the material benefits that we might get from תורה <i>Torah</i> that are transactional in nature."], [44.92, "תורה <i>Torah</i> is the framework for life."], [45.28, "It is what brings us to our highest selves and the highest values."], [52.24, "They are the very ideals that embolden the soul and connect us with God."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-orbTI13h-tE", "title": "Learn from All | Pearls From Pirke Abot #11", "short_title": "Learn from All", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 11, "blurb": "You can learn from anyone, just give them a chance!", "date": "2026-05-20", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-orbTI13h-tE.mp3", "duration": 52, "audio_local": "audio/001-orbTI13h-tE.mp3", "transcript": "בן זומא <i>Ben Zoma</i> says איזהו חכם <i>Eizehu Chacham</i>, who is a wise person, הלומד מכל אדם <i>Halomed Mikol Adam</i>, a person who learns from every human. It teaches us that if we are truly wise, we recognize that wisdom is to be gained from all human beings. The diversity of human beings, the richness and complexity of human beings teaches us things and because we are so complex and so rich in our behaviors, every single person has something to teach us. And in that is a humility, it's recognizing that if we truly wish to be wise, at its core is a humility and that we, no matter how much we've studied and how much we've learned, we can learn something from every person in the world.", "sentences": [[0.28, "בן זומא <i>Ben Zoma</i> says איזהו חכם <i>Eizehu Chacham</i>, who is a wise person, הלומד מכל אדם <i>Halomed Mikol Adam</i>, a person who learns from every human."], [7.18, "It teaches us that if we are truly wise, we recognize that wisdom is to be gained from all human beings."], [13.56, "The diversity of human beings, the richness and complexity of human beings teaches us things and because we are so complex and so rich in our behaviors, every single person has something to teach us."], [26.52, "And in that is a humility, it's recognizing that if we truly wish to be wise, at its core is a humility and that we, no matter how much we've studied and how much we've learned, we can learn something from every person in the world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-9yWAntirYZQ", "title": "Price of MITSVOT | Pearls From Pirke Abot #9", "short_title": "Price of MITSVOT", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 9, "blurb": "In the repertoire of positive mitsvot, there are some that may seem to us to be lighter or less significant than others...", "date": "2026-05-14", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-9yWAntirYZQ.mp3", "duration": 101, "audio_local": "audio/001-9yWAntirYZQ.mp3", "transcript": "There are positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> and negative מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> in the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the do's and the don'ts, and the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> that tell us to do things for God or in service of God, are really at the core of our relationship with Him, because the negative ones, the ones that tell us don't do this, is just to keep us in line, but the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are actually the acts of love that we do in our engagement with God. And in the repertoire of positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, there are some that may seem to us to be lighter or less significant than others, and רבי יהודה הנשיא <i>Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi</i> says, don't make evaluation judgments like that on the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>. Seek all of them, do all of them equally. We really have no idea what the implications of the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are, what the things that we do in our relationship with God, how it is that they affect and manifest in terms of the relationship. And the reason for that is because it's not just a task-oriented thing, right? We know in the important relationships in our lives that we're not just looking for tasks to be accomplished. We really want the actions to be indicative of the way that we approach the intentions that we have to the relationship itself. And so, it's possible that a small thoughtful note in a close and loving relationship may be more important than a full anniversary dinner. And so we never really know what a particular deed within the full context of the relationship is going to mean. Care about all of them and relate to all of them equally.", "sentences": [[0.0, "There are positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> and negative מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> in the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the do's and the don'ts, and the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> that tell us to do things for God or in service of God, are really at the core of our relationship with Him, because the negative ones, the ones that tell us don't do this, is just to keep us in line, but the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are actually the acts of love that we do in our engagement with God."], [25.28, "And in the repertoire of positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, there are some that may seem to us to be lighter or less significant than others, and רבי יהודה הנשיא <i>Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi</i> says, don't make evaluation judgments like that on the positive מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>."], [35.66, "Seek all of them, do all of them equally."], [40.46, "We really have no idea what the implications of the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are, what the things that we do in our relationship with God, how it is that they affect and manifest in terms of the relationship."], [52.98, "And the reason for that is because it's not just a task-oriented thing, right?"], [55.76, "We know in the important relationships in our lives that we're not just looking for tasks to be accomplished."], [61.46, "We really want the actions to be indicative of the way that we approach the intentions that we have to the relationship itself."], [68.6, "And so, it's possible that a small thoughtful note in a close and loving relationship may be more important than a full anniversary dinner."], [78.54, "And so we never really know what a particular deed within the full context of the relationship is going to mean."], [82.96, "Care about all of them and relate to all of them equally."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-g0sfzV-HyN0", "title": "Be INVOLVED in the World | Pearls From Pirke Abot #8", "short_title": "Be INVOLVED in the World", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 8, "blurb": "We should spend a great deal of our time studying the Torah, but we should do it together with our involvement in the world.", "date": "2026-05-13", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-g0sfzV-HyN0.mp3", "duration": 75, "audio_local": "audio/001-g0sfzV-HyN0.mp3", "transcript": "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> said that we should really be careful not to only study תורה <i>Torah</i> or only be involved in the world. What he says is יפה תלמוד תורה עם דרך ארץ <i>Yaffa Talmud Torah im Derech Eretz</i>, that both the study of תורה <i>Torah</i> and our engagement in the regular runnings of the world are beautiful when they are together, שיגיעת שניהם משכחת עוון <i>she-yegiat shneihem mashkachat avon</i>, because the exertion in both of them removes from us עוון <i>avon</i>. עוון <i>Avon</i> literally means corruption from the word עיוות <i>ivut</i>, which essentially is corruption. So these things are important because if we spend all of our time in the study but we don't really engage in the practical implications of how that study manifests in the real world, we've removed ourselves from the world. And if we only engage in the world and we really don't have any study, we have no real way of knowing what is the best way to navigate the world. So we should spend a great deal of our time studying the תורה <i>Torah</i>, but we should do it together with our involvement in the world so that all of the theory that we study can find beautiful manifestation in practice.", "sentences": [[0.82, "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> said that we should really be careful not to only study תורה <i>Torah</i> or only be involved in the world."], [10.16, "What he says is יפה תלמוד תורה עם דרך ארץ <i>Yaffa Talmud Torah im Derech Eretz</i>, that both the study of תורה <i>Torah</i> and our engagement in the regular runnings of the world are beautiful when they are together, שיגיעת שניהם משכחת עוון <i>she-yegiat shneihem mashkachat avon</i>, because the exertion in both of them removes from us עוון <i>avon</i>."], [24.54, "עוון <i>Avon</i> literally means corruption from the word עיוות <i>ivut</i>, which essentially is corruption."], [27.36, "So these things are important because if we spend all of our time in the study but we don't really engage in the practical implications of how that study manifests in the real world, we've removed ourselves from the world."], [40.86, "And if we only engage in the world and we really don't have any study, we have no real way of knowing what is the best way to navigate the world."], [51.12, "So we should spend a great deal of our time studying the תורה <i>Torah</i>, but we should do it together with our involvement in the world so that all of the theory that we study can find beautiful manifestation in practice."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-Rm9bpo3SBUY", "title": "CONNECT With People in Charge | Pearls From Pirke Abot #7", "short_title": "CONNECT With People in Charge", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 7, "blurb": "You have to be very careful when you are connecting with and getting in with people who are in charge...", "date": "2026-05-11", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-Rm9bpo3SBUY.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/001-Rm9bpo3SBUY.mp3", "transcript": "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says something interesting about connecting with people in charge, and he says usually you have to be very careful when you are connecting with and getting in with people who are in charge, because nine times out of ten it's transactional, and what they're looking for is something that can serve their purposes and what you're looking for is a way to get ahead. And so in the transactional relationship it is entirely based on needs; when they can use you, you're held close, but when you're no longer needed, you are dropped. Instead, seek relationships that look for real bonding, in which you as a person are valuable and not just what it is that you give or what it is that you can provide. And so רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says whenever you're connecting with people who are in charge, you've got to be careful about the transactional nature.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says something interesting about connecting with people in charge, and he says usually you have to be very careful when you are connecting with and getting in with people who are in charge, because nine times out of ten it's transactional, and what they're looking for is something that can serve their purposes and what you're looking for is a way to get ahead."], [24.88, "And so in the transactional relationship it is entirely based on needs; when they can use you, you're held close, but when you're no longer needed, you are dropped."], [34.22, "Instead, seek relationships that look for real bonding, in which you as a person are valuable and not just what it is that you give or what it is that you can provide."], [47.1, "And so רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says whenever you're connecting with people who are in charge, you've got to be careful about the transactional nature."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-VQD3Y9C2uD8", "title": "DON'T Be Bashful | Pearls From Pirke Abot #6", "short_title": "DON'T Be Bashful", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 6, "blurb": "To be able to learn and teach, you MUST be able to speak up.", "date": "2026-05-08", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-VQD3Y9C2uD8.mp3", "duration": 70, "audio_local": "audio/001-VQD3Y9C2uD8.mp3", "transcript": "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says לא הבישן למד <i>Lo habayshan lamed</i> ולא הקפדן מלמד <i>v'lo hakapdan melamed</i>. That a bashful person can never really learn and an insistent person, a person who's always concerned about whether a person is doing appropriately or not appropriately, they call people on every little thing that they do. And he says that when it comes to study, when it comes to learning, when it comes to teaching, those attributes have no place. Not only do they have no place, they are detrimental. My own sense of confidence in being able to say something or a person's insistence on having to call people on their mistakes, those things fly in the face of being able to actually engage in an endeavor of wisdom and understanding. And that what we need to do is put those things aside if we really do care about learning. And we cannot allow those human dispositions to get in the way of what must be most precious, and that is knowledge, wisdom, understanding. And so we do have to try to get over those attributes if we really wish to learn.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבן גמליאל <i>Rabban Gamliel</i> says לא הבישן למד <i>Lo habayshan lamed</i> ולא הקפדן מלמד <i>v'lo hakapdan melamed</i>."], [4.36, "That a bashful person can never really learn and an insistent person, a person who's always concerned about whether a person is doing appropriately or not appropriately, they call people on every little thing that they do."], [17.16, "And he says that when it comes to study, when it comes to learning, when it comes to teaching, those attributes have no place."], [24.44, "Not only do they have no place, they are detrimental."], [27.25, "My own sense of confidence in being able to say something or a person's insistence on having to call people on their mistakes, those things fly in the face of being able to actually engage in an endeavor of wisdom and understanding."], [42.12, "And that what we need to do is put those things aside if we really do care about learning."], [48.7, "And we cannot allow those human dispositions to get in the way of what must be most precious, and that is knowledge, wisdom, understanding."], [58.22, "And so we do have to try to get over those attributes if we really wish to learn."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-KmSMXfihkYQ", "title": "Don't Close Your DOOR | Pearls From Pirke Abot #5", "short_title": "Don't Close Your DOOR", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 5, "blurb": "Your home should be a place that provides what people might need...", "date": "2026-05-06", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/064-KmSMXfihkYQ.mp3", "duration": 61, "audio_local": "audio/064-KmSMXfihkYQ.mp3", "transcript": "יוסי בן יוחנן <i>Yose ben Yochanan</i> says יהי ביתך פתוח לרווחה <i>Yehi beitcha patuach lervacha</i>, which means your home should be a place that is open wide. And what that's suggesting is your home should be a place that provides what people might need, that gives. And so, although of course it has to do with people that are poor, that don't have enough, that of course people like that should be invited into your house and welcomed at your table, it goes beyond that. It goes towards thinking who might need a place to stay, a meal for שבת <i>Shabbat</i>? Who might need a place to feel at home that might not have that? If you can provide that for other people in your home in a way that is safe and inviting, do so, because not only does it provide for them, it enhances you and your life.", "sentences": [[0.0, "יוסי בן יוחנן <i>Yose ben Yochanan</i> says יהי ביתך פתוח לרווחה <i>Yehi beitcha patuach lervacha</i>, which means your home should be a place that is open wide."], [6.7, "And what that's suggesting is your home should be a place that provides what people might need, that gives."], [12.74, "And so, although of course it has to do with people that are poor, that don't have enough, that of course people like that should be invited into your house and welcomed at your table, it goes beyond that."], [23.28, "It goes towards thinking who might need a place to stay, a meal for שבת <i>Shabbat</i>?"], [28.8, "Who might need a place to feel at home that might not have that?"], [33.66, "If you can provide that for other people in your home in a way that is safe and inviting, do so, because not only does it provide for them, it enhances you and your life."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-Nw4tj2PrhNs", "title": "You HAVEN'T Seen This | Pearls From Pirke Abot #4", "short_title": "You HAVEN'T Seen This", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 4, "blurb": "Before you judge, consider the biases that you may have...", "date": "2026-05-05", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-Nw4tj2PrhNs.mp3", "duration": 67, "audio_local": "audio/001-Nw4tj2PrhNs.mp3", "transcript": "The אנשי כנסת הגדולה <i>Anshei Knesset HaGedolah</i> said, הוו מתונים בדין <i>Hevu metunim badin</i>. Pause in judgment. Before you judge, consider the biases that you may have. And as רבינו עובדיה ברטנורא <i>Rabbeinu Ovadia Bartenura</i> explains, what this is essentially saying to us is if you see something that you've seen or you think you've seen once, twice, three times, never say, I've already seen this once, twice, or three times. Always pause and consider the fact that there is something new here. הוו מתונים בדין <i>Hevu metunim badin</i> means pause, wait. Look at this fresh. Come to it with the openness of a beginner. Always question what is new here, what might I not be seeing that I think I've already seen. And in doing so, it allows us for our biases to drop and for our openness to new things and real truths to emerge.", "sentences": [[0.68, "The אנשי כנסת הגדולה <i>Anshei Knesset HaGedolah</i> said, הוו מתונים בדין <i>Hevu metunim badin</i>."], [4.44, "Pause in judgment."], [6.72, "Before you judge, consider the biases that you may have."], [11.64, "And as רבינו עובדיה ברטנורא <i>Rabbeinu Ovadia Bartenura</i> explains, what this is essentially saying to us is if you see something that you've seen or you think you've seen once, twice, three times, never say, I've already seen this once, twice, or three times."], [25.5, "Always pause and consider the fact that there is something new here."], [31.34, "הוו מתונים בדין <i>Hevu metunim badin</i> means pause, wait."], [34.24, "Look at this fresh."], [36.48, "Come to it with the openness of a beginner."], [39.74, "Always question what is new here, what might I not be seeing that I think I've already seen."], [46.8, "And in doing so, it allows us for our biases to drop and for our openness to new things and real truths to emerge."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-A4AVr7lrFcA", "title": "Think Like Them | Pearls From Pirke Abot #3", "short_title": "Think Like Them", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 3, "blurb": "Contrary to popular belief, we don't think for ourselves, no human being really thinks entirely for themselves...", "date": "2026-05-04", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-A4AVr7lrFcA.mp3", "duration": 63, "audio_local": "audio/001-A4AVr7lrFcA.mp3", "transcript": "יוסי בן יועזר <i>Yose ben Yoezer</i> says that your house should be open to חכמים <i>Chachamim</i>. יהי ביתך בית ועד לחכמים <i>Yehi beitcha beit vaad lachachamim</i>. Let your home be a place that wise people, that the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> gather. And the reason for that is because, contrary to popular belief, we don't think for ourselves. No human being really thinks entirely for themselves. We are a social species and we are influenced by the thinking of others. And therefore, יוסי בן יועזר <i>Yose ben Yoezer</i> says, if you want to ensure that your thought is healthy and strong, have חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> who have trained themselves and have involved themselves to think critically, clearly, and without bias, to be the members of your household, because when you surround yourself with them, you begin to think like them, and that is who it is that we want to think like.", "sentences": [[0.0, "יוסי בן יועזר <i>Yose ben Yoezer</i> says that your house should be open to חכמים <i>Chachamim</i>."], [5.14, "יהי ביתך בית ועד לחכמים <i>Yehi beitcha beit vaad lachachamim</i>."], [6.3, "Let your home be a place that wise people, that the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> gather."], [10.64, "And the reason for that is because, contrary to popular belief, we don't think for ourselves."], [16.74, "No human being really thinks entirely for themselves."], [16.92, "We are a social species and we are influenced by the thinking of others."], [22.62, "And therefore, יוסי בן יועזר <i>Yose ben Yoezer</i> says, if you want to ensure that your thought is healthy and strong, have חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> who have trained themselves and have involved themselves to think critically, clearly, and without bias, to be the members of your household, because when you surround yourself with them, you begin to think like them, and that is who it is that we want to think like."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-p3UV9U6WhA4", "title": "Transactional RELATIONSHIPS | Pearls From Pirke Abot #2", "short_title": "Transactional RELATIONSHIPS", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 2, "blurb": "the way that we serve God should not be transactional.", "date": "2026-04-29", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-p3UV9U6WhA4.mp3", "duration": 116, "audio_local": "audio/001-p3UV9U6WhA4.mp3", "transcript": "אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> said don't be כעבד המשמש רבו על מנת לקבל פרס <i>Ke'eved hameshammesh rabbo al menat lekabel pras</i>. Do not serve the master in order to get a prize for it. Rather do so for no prize. Essentially what אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> is saying is that the way that we serve God should not be transactional. And what is the difference between a transactional relationship and a genuinely loving relationship, which is what our relationship with God is meant to be? A transactional relationship always is tied and lives on what is being provided. If I can provide something for the person and the relationship is intact, it's transactional. They provide for me, and that's why the relationship holds, it's transactional. But if the providing of things ceases, if I can no longer give or if they can no longer give to me, does the relationship hold? A relationship that is love-based as opposed to transactional is a relationship that lives because of who you are, not because of what it is that you give to me or what it is that you do for me, and vice versa. It is because of who I am to you, as opposed to what it is that I give to you or do for you. And so when those things go away, does the relationship maintain because of the value of who you are? And when it comes to God, that of course is how our relationship is meant to be. I am in relationship with God because of who He is, not because of what He does for me. And even if those things were to cease, the value of the relationship, of being close to Him because of who He is, the source of all life, the maker of the universe, that to me is valuable and that is how אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> says our relationship should be.", "sentences": [[0.0, "אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> said don't be כעבד המשמש רבו על מנת לקבל פרס <i>Ke'eved hameshammesh rabbo al menat lekabel pras</i>."], [4.88, "Do not serve the master in order to get a prize for it."], [7.38, "Rather do so for no prize."], [8.94, "Essentially what אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> is saying is that the way that we serve God should not be transactional."], [15.82, "And what is the difference between a transactional relationship and a genuinely loving relationship, which is what our relationship with God is meant to be?"], [25.64, "A transactional relationship always is tied and lives on what is being provided."], [30.18, "If I can provide something for the person and the relationship is intact, it's transactional."], [33.78, "They provide for me, and that's why the relationship holds, it's transactional."], [38.74, "But if the providing of things ceases, if I can no longer give or if they can no longer give to me, does the relationship hold?"], [48.28, "A relationship that is love-based as opposed to transactional is a relationship that lives because of who you are, not because of what it is that you give to me or what it is that you do for me, and vice versa."], [64.12, "It is because of who I am to you, as opposed to what it is that I give to you or do for you."], [68.26, "And so when those things go away, does the relationship maintain because of the value of who you are?"], [72.5, "And when it comes to God, that of course is how our relationship is meant to be."], [75.34, "I am in relationship with God because of who He is, not because of what He does for me."], [81.26, "And even if those things were to cease, the value of the relationship, of being close to Him because of who He is, the source of all life, the maker of the universe, that to me is valuable and that is how אנטיגנוס איש סוכו <i>Antignos Ish Socho</i> says our relationship should be."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-FrpP9aLmplc", "title": "What We Can Learn From the CAMEL | Pearls From Pirke Abot #1", "short_title": "What We Can Learn From the CAMEL", "series": "Pearls From Pirke Abot", "number": 1, "blurb": "What is gemilut hasidim?", "date": "2026-04-28", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/001-FrpP9aLmplc.mp3", "duration": 110, "audio_local": "audio/001-FrpP9aLmplc.mp3", "transcript": "שמעון הצדיק <i>Shimon HaTzadik</i> says that גמילות חסדים <i>Gemilut Chasadim</i>, that doing kindness is one of the three pillars upon which the world stands. But what is גמילות חסדים <i>Gemilut Chasadim</i>? It's more than just doing kindness. The word גמילות <i>Gemilut</i> comes from the root גמל <i>Gamal</i>, which literally means to wean or weaning. And what that essentially is, is bringing someone from dependency on something towards independence, towards being able to stand on their own two feet. So when a baby is weaned from mother's milk, they are now able to digest their own food and to begin processing their own food. But the concept is one that runs throughout life. We move from dependence towards independence. The reason we call camels גמל <i>Gamal</i> is because we see them as these massive weaned beasts that are able to traverse mass expanses of desert without needing water too often because they've got what they need on their backs. And doing kindness in the world, we have to be sensitive to always do it in a way that preserves the dignity and independence of the one receiving it. Otherwise, we fall into two problematic extremes. Either we just don't help people and say it's their problem, when the world really falls apart when we act in that way. And the second is when we help people but maintain their dependency on the help so that they really never detach and ultimately achieve independence but where there is always a need that is never filled. שמעון הצדיק <i>Shimon HaTzadik</i> says true kindness is גמילות חסד <i>Gemilut Chesed</i>, is the doing of kindness in a way that weans a person to independence.", "sentences": [[0.0, "שמעון הצדיק <i>Shimon HaTzadik</i> says that גמילות חסדים <i>Gemilut Chasadim</i>, that doing kindness is one of the three pillars upon which the world stands."], [8.2, "But what is גמילות חסדים <i>Gemilut Chasadim</i>?"], [10.32, "It's more than just doing kindness."], [12.36, "The word גמילות <i>Gemilut</i> comes from the root גמל <i>Gamal</i>, which literally means to wean or weaning."], [18.48, "And what that essentially is, is bringing someone from dependency on something towards independence, towards being able to stand on their own two feet."], [28.66, "So when a baby is weaned from mother's milk, they are now able to digest their own food and to begin processing their own food."], [35.88, "But the concept is one that runs throughout life."], [38.82, "We move from dependence towards independence."], [42.18, "The reason we call camels גמל <i>Gamal</i> is because we see them as these massive weaned beasts that are able to traverse mass expanses of desert without needing water too often because they've got what they need on their backs."], [56.06, "And doing kindness in the world, we have to be sensitive to always do it in a way that preserves the dignity and independence of the one receiving it."], [66.96, "Otherwise, we fall into two problematic extremes."], [70.5, "Either we just don't help people and say it's their problem, when the world really falls apart when we act in that way."], [77.78, "And the second is when we help people but maintain their dependency on the help so that they really never detach and ultimately achieve independence but where there is always a need that is never filled."], [91.32, "שמעון הצדיק <i>Shimon HaTzadik</i> says true kindness is גמילות חסד <i>Gemilut Chesed</i>, is the doing of kindness in a way that weans a person to independence."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-NkmbZzpMGLU", "title": "Live Life ACROSS Generations | Thought For the Day #54", "short_title": "Live Life ACROSS Generations", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 54, "blurb": "When you have older friends,\n\nyou gain depth,\n\nyou gain perspective,\n\nyou gain hard earned wisdom.", "date": "2026-04-13", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/003-NkmbZzpMGLU.mp3", "duration": 83, "audio_local": "audio/003-NkmbZzpMGLU.mp3", "transcript": "One of the quiet mistakes that we make in life is that we segregate by age. We surround ourselves only with the people that are in our peer group or our age group. So they're people that are basically at the same stage in life, usually have the same concerns, and also the same perspective. But life is meant to be lived across generations. When you have older friends, you gain depth, you gain perspective, you gain hard-earned wisdom. You have a sense of where things in your life may lead, and that's invaluable. When you have younger friends, you stay connected to movement, change, and where the consciousness of the world is emerging. You have your finger on the pulse of now. And something else happens. When you grow older with friends of different generations, you're far less likely to become isolated because you've already built relationships that reach far beyond your stage of life. A great piece of advice is to strive to have friends in every decade of life. That way, wherever it is that you might be in your life, you always can have connection to the full experience of life in all of its stages.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the quiet mistakes that we make in life is that we segregate by age."], [4.8, "We surround ourselves only with the people that are in our peer group or our age group."], [10.58, "So they're people that are basically at the same stage in life, usually have the same concerns, and also the same perspective."], [17.56, "But life is meant to be lived across generations."], [20.38, "When you have older friends, you gain depth, you gain perspective, you gain hard-earned wisdom."], [25.42, "You have a sense of where things in your life may lead, and that's invaluable."], [30.88, "When you have younger friends, you stay connected to movement, change, and where the consciousness of the world is emerging."], [38.32, "You have your finger on the pulse of now."], [41.9, "And something else happens."], [43.4, "When you grow older with friends of different generations, you're far less likely to become isolated because you've already built relationships that reach far beyond your stage of life."], [55.58, "A great piece of advice is to strive to have friends in every decade of life."], [62.94, "That way, wherever it is that you might be in your life, you always can have connection to the full experience of life in all of its stages."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd--Fqt6T1yRfs", "title": "What is This Coming to TEACH Us? | Thought For the Day #55", "short_title": "What is This Coming to TEACH Us?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 55, "blurb": "Not everything in Torah is meant to be taken literally.", "date": "2026-04-13", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/002--Fqt6T1yRfs.mp3", "duration": 75, "audio_local": "audio/002--Fqt6T1yRfs.mp3", "transcript": "Not everything in תורה <i>Torah</i> is meant to be taken literally, especially when it comes to what we call אגדה <i>Aggada</i>, which are the parables of the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> that tell sometimes quite outlandish stories. The חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> say אין מקשין על הדרש <i>ein makshin al hadrash</i>, which essentially means that we are not to draw conclusions, build arguments or proofs from the parables, from the אגדות <i>aggadot</i> that the חכמים <i>hachamim</i> bring in the teaching of תורה <i>Torah</i>. אגדה <i>Aggada</i> speaks to us in a different language than usual. It speaks to us in metaphor and in parables, in order to be able to teach us deep truths that are not easily presented in simple prose. הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>, רב האי גאון <i>Rav Hai Gaon</i>, and others all put this forward to us. In fact, אבן עזרא <i>Ibn Ezra</i> at the end of his introduction to the תורה <i>Torah</i> says that if we start trying to explain דרש <i>derash</i>, there is no end to it. מדרש <i>Midrash</i> is there to open your thinking. It is never the question when we study מדרש <i>midrash</i>, did this literally happen? Rather, the question is always, what is this coming to teach us?", "sentences": [[0.0, "Not everything in תורה <i>Torah</i> is meant to be taken literally, especially when it comes to what we call אגדה <i>Aggada</i>, which are the parables of the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> that tell sometimes quite outlandish stories."], [12.62, "The חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> say אין מקשין על הדרש <i>ein makshin al hadrash</i>, which essentially means that we are not to draw conclusions, build arguments or proofs from the parables, from the אגדות <i>aggadot</i> that the חכמים <i>hachamim</i> bring in the teaching of תורה <i>Torah</i>."], [24.1, "אגדה <i>Aggada</i> speaks to us in a different language than usual."], [26.74, "It speaks to us in metaphor and in parables, in order to be able to teach us deep truths that are not easily presented in simple prose."], [32.44, "הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>, רב האי גאון <i>Rav Hai Gaon</i>, and others all put this forward to us."], [36.38, "In fact, אבן עזרא <i>Ibn Ezra</i> at the end of his introduction to the תורה <i>Torah</i> says that if we start trying to explain דרש <i>derash</i>, there is no end to it."], [44.44, "מדרש <i>Midrash</i> is there to open your thinking."], [46.32, "It is never the question when we study מדרש <i>midrash</i>, did this literally happen?"], [50.48, "Rather, the question is always, what is this coming to teach us?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-PUCdA2aSPkM", "title": "Happiness is NOW | Thought For the Day #53", "short_title": "Happiness is NOW", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 53, "blurb": "There's a concept in psychology called hedonic adaptation...", "date": "2026-04-06", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/004-PUCdA2aSPkM.mp3", "duration": 95, "audio_local": "audio/004-PUCdA2aSPkM.mp3", "transcript": "There's a tendency that we have to postpone happiness. We say we will be happy when and we look forward to some event that'll happen in the future that will finally bring us happiness. But that's a result of not being present and grateful for what it is that we have right now. One of the beautiful things about prayer is that it focuses on gratitude, that we recognize our lives for what it is that we have. There's a concept in psychology called hedonic adaptation, which basically means we get used to the good things that we have. One way to orient ourselves to be able to really appreciate what we have is to think about what it would be like if we didn't have it, just to consider what life might be like if we didn't have what we already have, rather than thinking about what the future will be like when we have this next thing that we're looking for. Now that's not to say that we shouldn't have aspirations, but there's something very special and very meaningful about being able to recognize and be grateful for what it is that we have today.", "sentences": [[0.0, "There's a tendency that we have to postpone happiness."], [3.64, "We say we will be happy when and we look forward to some event that'll happen in the future that will finally bring us happiness."], [12.06, "But that's a result of not being present and grateful for what it is that we have right now."], [19.6, "One of the beautiful things about prayer is that it focuses on gratitude, that we recognize our lives for what it is that we have."], [28.28, "There's a concept in psychology called hedonic adaptation, which basically means we get used to the good things that we have."], [36.16, "One way to orient ourselves to be able to really appreciate what we have is to think about what it would be like if we didn't have it, just to consider what life might be like if we didn't have what we already have, rather than thinking about what the future will be like when we have this next thing that we're looking for."], [56.72, "Now that's not to say that we shouldn't have aspirations, but there's something very special and very meaningful about being able to recognize and be grateful for what it is that we have today."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-8LiG5Kwmo_E", "title": "Listen, DON'T Fix | Thought For the Day #52", "short_title": "Listen, DON'T Fix", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 52, "blurb": "When a person is distraught or distressed, one of the most important things that we could do is to be present for them.", "date": "2026-03-31", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/005-8LiG5Kwmo_E.mp3", "duration": 52, "audio_local": "audio/005-8LiG5Kwmo_E.mp3", "transcript": "When we hear that someone is in distress or in trouble or having a difficult time, we often have a tendency to want to see if we can fix the things that are problematic for them. But more often than not, what's really needed is to listen, to hold space for the person. When a person is distraught or distressed, one of the most important things that we can do is to be present for them, to see them, to hear them and recognize that they are going through a difficult time. It's for this reason that in the laws of mourning in the תורה <i>Torah</i>, on the first days of mourning, we don't even say anything when we visit. What we do is be with them, we sit with them, we provide presence and hold space for them. That has a much deeper level of healing than we often anticipate.", "sentences": [[0.0, "When we hear that someone is in distress or in trouble or having a difficult time, we often have a tendency to want to see if we can fix the things that are problematic for them."], [10.54, "But more often than not, what's really needed is to listen, to hold space for the person."], [15.74, "When a person is distraught or distressed, one of the most important things that we can do is to be present for them, to see them, to hear them and recognize that they are going through a difficult time."], [25.88, "It's for this reason that in the laws of mourning in the תורה <i>Torah</i>, on the first days of mourning, we don't even say anything when we visit."], [33.6, "What we do is be with them, we sit with them, we provide presence and hold space for them."], [41.58, "That has a much deeper level of healing than we often anticipate."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-X82lcPZal1Q", "title": "Loving LIFE & SADNESS | Thought For the Day #51", "short_title": "Loving LIFE & SADNESS", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 51, "blurb": "Loving your life and being appreciative of your life doesn't mean that you're just happy all the time.", "date": "2026-03-29", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/006-X82lcPZal1Q.mp3", "duration": 70, "audio_local": "audio/006-X82lcPZal1Q.mp3", "transcript": "You can love your life and still be sad sometimes. Loving your life and being appreciative of your life doesn't mean that you're just happy all the time. It means that overall you do recognize the beauty of the life that you have, but emotional range, the ability to be sad, angry, distraught, worried, also means that you have a sensitivity to reality, to what's actually going on in the world. It doesn't mean that you don't appreciate what it is that you have, it does mean that you are aware of, respond to, and engage with the nature of reality. And one of the things that we see with the characters of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the characters throughout the תנך <i>Tanakh</i>, is that the great characters of the תנך <i>Tanakh</i> have ranges of emotions. They get happy and sad and angry and respond to reality as they are alive and full. Doesn't mean that they weren't happy about their lives, that they didn't thank God for the lives that they had. It meant that they were living their lives and feeling and experiencing the full range of what it means to live in this world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "You can love your life and still be sad sometimes."], [3.48, "Loving your life and being appreciative of your life doesn't mean that you're just happy all the time."], [8.36, "It means that overall you do recognize the beauty of the life that you have, but emotional range, the ability to be sad, angry, distraught, worried, also means that you have a sensitivity to reality, to what's actually going on in the world."], [23.86, "It doesn't mean that you don't appreciate what it is that you have, it does mean that you are aware of, respond to, and engage with the nature of reality."], [33.12, "And one of the things that we see with the characters of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the characters throughout the תנך <i>Tanakh</i>, is that the great characters of the תנך <i>Tanakh</i> have ranges of emotions."], [41.58, "They get happy and sad and angry and respond to reality as they are alive and full."], [48.46, "Doesn't mean that they weren't happy about their lives, that they didn't thank God for the lives that they had."], [52.66, "It meant that they were living their lives and feeling and experiencing the full range of what it means to live in this world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-pbgnCdTBZNQ", "title": "PAUSE and Watch | Thought For the Day #50", "short_title": "PAUSE and Watch", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 50, "blurb": "One thing that people don't always realize is that when they think about self-expression, they're often thinking really about ego expression.", "date": "2026-03-26", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/007-pbgnCdTBZNQ.mp3", "duration": 102, "audio_local": "audio/007-pbgnCdTBZNQ.mp3", "transcript": "One of the difficulties of the human experience is that we are meaning-seeking beings. And so, without even thinking about it, we give meaning to almost everything that we experience: to our thoughts, to our feelings, and to the events in the world. So when I have a thought, I don't just see the thought, I immediately decide what the thought means. If I have a feeling, for example, if I'm feeling sad, I already think about what it means to feel sad. But the feeling is not the same as the value. The thought is not the same as the value. An event in the world is not the same as the meaning that we put onto it. And we do this so quickly that we become the victims of whatever value it is that we put onto something, and we live within those judgments. A stronger and more fortified way to live is to practice pausing between the thought, the feeling, the event, and the judgment we make about it. We can learn to observe the thought that comes into our head as though we're watching it pass through, to observe the feeling that we have rather than deciding what it is that it means to us, to observe the events that are going on around us before we decide what we think about them. Living that way gives us much more strength than being carried away with the knee-jerk judgments that our minds make.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the difficulties of the human experience is that we are meaning-seeking beings."], [6.66, "And so, without even thinking about it, we give meaning to almost everything that we experience: to our thoughts, to our feelings, and to the events in the world."], [17.04, "So when I have a thought, I don't just see the thought, I immediately decide what the thought means."], [23.86, "If I have a feeling, for example, if I'm feeling sad, I already think about what it means to feel sad."], [30.58, "But the feeling is not the same as the value."], [35.14, "The thought is not the same as the value."], [37.7, "An event in the world is not the same as the meaning that we put onto it."], [42.4, "And we do this so quickly that we become the victims of whatever value it is that we put onto something, and we live within those judgments."], [51.46, "A stronger and more fortified way to live is to practice pausing between the thought, the feeling, the event, and the judgment we make about it."], [66.0, "We can learn to observe the thought that comes into our head as though we're watching it pass through, to observe the feeling that we have rather than deciding what it is that it means to us, to observe the events that are going on around us before we decide what we think about them."], [83.36, "Living that way gives us much more strength than being carried away with the knee-jerk judgments that our minds make."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-vVjjXBikbjc", "title": "Is SELF EXPRESSION Good? | Thought For the Day #49", "short_title": "Is SELF EXPRESSION Good?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 49, "blurb": "One thing that people don't always realize is that when they think about self-expression, they're often thinking really about ego expression.", "date": "2026-03-24", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/008-vVjjXBikbjc.mp3", "duration": 78, "audio_local": "audio/008-vVjjXBikbjc.mp3", "transcript": "People talk about self-expression all the time as though it's a great thing. Express yourself, be who you are. But one thing that people don't always realize is that when they think about self-expression, they're thinking really about ego expression rather than self-expression. And there's a huge difference. What is the ego? The ego is the part of ourselves that is biologically driven to survive. And that part of ourselves is old and it is shared across species. It's the part of ourselves that senses life as a zero-sum game. It is either eat or be eaten, kill or be killed, survive or die. Even if it means that I need to undermine someone in order for me to be able to be better, or if I need to make light of someone in order for me to be valued. Those are ego-oriented things. Even things that seem to be altruistic, like making friends in order for me to be able to be in a better social position, those are all ego things. And expression from that part of self is not self-expression, it's ego expression. Before you decide to express yourself, question: is this coming from my ego or is it coming from my true self?", "sentences": [[0.0, "People talk about self-expression all the time as though it's a great thing."], [4.02, "Express yourself, be who you are."], [6.04, "But one thing that people don't always realize is that when they think about self-expression, they're thinking really about ego expression rather than self-expression."], [14.3, "And there's a huge difference."], [15.62, "What is the ego?"], [16.9, "The ego is the part of ourselves that is biologically driven to survive."], [21.36, "And that part of ourselves is old and it is shared across species."], [26.72, "It's the part of ourselves that senses life as a zero-sum game."], [31.82, "It is either eat or be eaten, kill or be killed, survive or die."], [35.44, "Even if it means that I need to undermine someone in order for me to be able to be better, or if I need to make light of someone in order for me to be valued."], [44.24, "Those are ego-oriented things."], [46.58, "Even things that seem to be altruistic, like making friends in order for me to be able to be in a better social position, those are all ego things."], [55.02, "And expression from that part of self is not self-expression, it's ego expression."], [60.76, "Before you decide to express yourself, question: is this coming from my ego or is it coming from my true self?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-AoKEXaOQAHE", "title": "WHY Didn't the Torah Abolish Slavery? | Thought For the Day #48", "short_title": "WHY Didn't the Torah Abolish Slavery?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 48, "blurb": "Why didn't the Torah just speak in ways that we would expect a modern ethical framework to be?", "date": "2026-03-19", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/009-AoKEXaOQAHE.mp3", "duration": 57, "audio_local": "audio/009-AoKEXaOQAHE.mp3", "transcript": "The תורה <i>Torah</i> was given thousands of years ago, and it spoke to an ancient civilization. Why didn't the תורה <i>Torah</i> just do away with slavery? Why didn't the תורה <i>Torah</i> just speak in ways that we would expect a modern ethical framework to be? Because the תורה <i>Torah</i> was given in a given society. The people of ישראל <i>Israel</i> were living in an already developed cultural system, and they couldn't have suddenly changed the entire system of their culture any more than they could have suddenly started speaking English. So the תורה <i>Torah</i> sets goals for us rather than fundamentally changing us in our place. It is aware that the best, most robust and substantial change occurs slowly over time. The תורה <i>Torah</i> recognizes that real change takes time.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> was given thousands of years ago, and it spoke to an ancient civilization."], [6.14, "Why didn't the תורה <i>Torah</i> just do away with slavery?"], [8.4, "Why didn't the תורה <i>Torah</i> just speak in ways that we would expect a modern ethical framework to be?"], [13.68, "Because the תורה <i>Torah</i> was given in a given society."], [17.02, "The people of ישראל <i>Israel</i> were living in an already developed cultural system, and they couldn't have suddenly changed the entire system of their culture any more than they could have suddenly started speaking English."], [27.58, "So the תורה <i>Torah</i> sets goals for us rather than fundamentally changing us in our place."], [33.3, "It is aware that the best, most robust and substantial change occurs slowly over time."], [41.88, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> recognizes that real change takes time."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-PBh_WUGe1_Y", "title": "The World Doesn't Need to Make SENSE | Thought For the Day #47", "short_title": "The World Doesn't Need to Make SENSE", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 47, "blurb": "We need to expand our thinking in order to be able to really understand what it is that's going on in the world.\n\nIt's also about recognizing that there are some things that we may just not have an ability to know.", "date": "2026-03-18", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/010-PBh_WUGe1_Y.mp3", "duration": 59, "audio_local": "audio/010-PBh_WUGe1_Y.mp3", "transcript": "A big mistake we often make is assuming that the world must make sense according to our thinking. The expensive word for that is epistemological narcissism, in which we believe that our thinking should be the way that the world makes sense to us. We need to expand our thinking in order to be able to really understand what it is that's going on in the world. And it's also about recognizing that there are some things that we may just not have an ability to know. The world is not meant to make sense to us. If anything does make sense to us, it's a remarkable aspect of living as a conscious being in this world, but we don't assume that it needs to make sense. We understand that all we've got is this brain in our heads and it may or may not help us understand our world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "A big mistake we often make is assuming that the world must make sense according to our thinking."], [6.12, "The expensive word for that is epistemological narcissism, in which we believe that our thinking should be the way that the world makes sense to us."], [16.5, "We need to expand our thinking in order to be able to really understand what it is that's going on in the world."], [22.56, "And it's also about recognizing that there are some things that we may just not have an ability to know."], [30.3, "The world is not meant to make sense to us."], [34.28, "If anything does make sense to us, it's a remarkable aspect of living as a conscious being in this world, but we don't assume that it needs to make sense."], [44.24, "We understand that all we've got is this brain in our heads and it may or may not help us understand our world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-XV9WKw7dkdk", "title": "I Left Egypt | Thought For the Day #46", "short_title": "I Left Egypt", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 46, "blurb": "We sit down at the Seder table and we talk about us leaving Egypt even though it was a 3000 year old event...", "date": "2026-03-17", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/011-XV9WKw7dkdk.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/011-XV9WKw7dkdk.mp3", "transcript": "The תורה <i>Torah</i> encourages us to see our identity embedded not only in our own memory, but in the memory of the ages. On Passover we sit down at the סדר <i>Seder</i> table and we talk about us leaving Egypt, even though it was a three thousand year old event. And the reason for that is because memory does not only live in the mind. The genome is also a kind of memory. It contains accumulated information of countless generations. Life is built on generations of stored knowledge. And so when we say something like, \\\"I know it in my bones,\\\" that's not too far off. When we say that, we are essentially saying that in my very being, in the cells of my body, in the DNA of who I am, are layers and layers of information that has been passed across generations. And that is why on Passover we say, \\\"I left Egypt.\\\" It happened to me.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> encourages us to see our identity embedded not only in our own memory, but in the memory of the ages."], [8.46, "On Passover we sit down at the סדר <i>Seder</i> table and we talk about us leaving Egypt, even though it was a three thousand year old event."], [16.94, "And the reason for that is because memory does not only live in the mind."], [21.5, "The genome is also a kind of memory."], [23.38, "It contains accumulated information of countless generations."], [27.78, "Life is built on generations of stored knowledge."], [31.92, "And so when we say something like, \\\"I know it in my bones,\\\" that's not too far off."], [36.98, "When we say that, we are essentially saying that in my very being, in the cells of my body, in the DNA of who I am, are layers and layers of information that has been passed across generations."], [51.78, "And that is why on Passover we say, \\\"I left Egypt.\\\" It happened to me."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-exLsLpnsZuY", "title": "Why We Love INFORMATION | Thought For the Day #45", "short_title": "Why We Love INFORMATION", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 45, "blurb": "the goal of learning is to get better information and through the information better understand what the nature of reality is...", "date": "2026-03-16", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/012-exLsLpnsZuY.mp3", "duration": 50, "audio_local": "audio/012-exLsLpnsZuY.mp3", "transcript": "The scientific definition of information is reduction of uncertainty. When we have information, we reduce uncertainty and we have more knowledge of what it is that is going on. And therefore, the goal of learning is not simply to gather facts, it is to better orient ourselves in reality. The goal of learning is to get better information, and through the information better understand what the nature of reality is. So we don't want to learn a lot of details, what we want is to understand how the details address what it is that we don't know.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The scientific definition of information is reduction of uncertainty."], [6.2, "When we have information, we reduce uncertainty and we have more knowledge of what it is that is going on."], [13.0, "And therefore, the goal of learning is not simply to gather facts, it is to better orient ourselves in reality."], [21.34, "The goal of learning is to get better information, and through the information better understand what the nature of reality is."], [31.24, "So we don't want to learn a lot of details, what we want is to understand how the details address what it is that we don't know."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-UMNGD157ihQ", "title": "Great Societies Start Early | Thought For the Day #44", "short_title": "Great Societies Start Early", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 44, "blurb": "We can't expect for the governments of the world to suddenly change if the childhood and the way that they grew up wasn't nourishing.", "date": "2026-03-15", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/013-UMNGD157ihQ.mp3", "duration": 52, "audio_local": "audio/013-UMNGD157ihQ.mp3", "transcript": "Psychologist Donald Winnicott pointed out that the emotional health of the world doesn't happen in government or in elections. It starts much earlier. It starts in how we raise our children. If we raise our children with confidence, with self-esteem, with secure attachment, with values and meaning, and we instill in them a healthy frame of the world, that carries on into adult society. We can't expect for the governments of the world to suddenly change if the childhood and the way that they grew up wasn't nourishing. That means that it is important how it is that we school our children, how we raise children, and the family structures in which we bring them up in the world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Psychologist Donald Winnicott pointed out that the emotional health of the world doesn't happen in government or in elections."], [7.12, "It starts much earlier."], [9.46, "It starts in how we raise our children."], [12.56, "If we raise our children with confidence, with self-esteem, with secure attachment, with values and meaning, and we instill in them a healthy frame of the world, that carries on into adult society."], [26.16, "We can't expect for the governments of the world to suddenly change if the childhood and the way that they grew up wasn't nourishing."], [35.26, "That means that it is important how it is that we school our children, how we raise children, and the family structures in which we bring them up in the world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-WGZBjbEQXF4", "title": "Most People Confuse THESE | Thought For the Day #42", "short_title": "Most People Confuse THESE", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 42, "blurb": "There is a big difference between a report and judgment...", "date": "2026-03-10", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/015-WGZBjbEQXF4.mp3", "duration": 72, "audio_local": "audio/015-WGZBjbEQXF4.mp3", "transcript": "It's important for us to know the difference and to detect the difference between report and judgment. A report is something that is objective. So if I say it's raining outside, you can check whether it is or not and verify that statement. That is a report. It is verifiable. It is objective. There are also judgments. Judgment is this is a beautiful car. Well, is it a beautiful car? How do you verify such a thing? I mean that has a great deal to do with what you're looking for in a car, your particular interests. That is a judgment, not a report. We hear judgments as though they are reports. It's important to tune our ear to recognize what is being said over here. Is it an objective fact or is it an opinion and a judgment? Opinions and judgments are fine, but it's important to recognize when they are opinions and judgments and to recognize the difference between those and objective facts like reports. In doing so, we have better clarity about how it is that we think and how we lead our lives.", "sentences": [[0.0, "It's important for us to know the difference and to detect the difference between report and judgment."], [5.8, "A report is something that is objective."], [8.52, "So if I say it's raining outside, you can check whether it is or not and verify that statement."], [15.6, "That is a report."], [16.52, "It is verifiable."], [17.74, "It is objective."], [19.02, "There are also judgments."], [20.88, "Judgment is this is a beautiful car."], [23.84, "Well, is it a beautiful car?"], [26.12, "How do you verify such a thing?"], [28.22, "I mean that has a great deal to do with what you're looking for in a car, your particular interests."], [33.76, "That is a judgment, not a report."], [36.88, "We hear judgments as though they are reports."], [40.42, "It's important to tune our ear to recognize what is being said over here."], [45.24, "Is it an objective fact or is it an opinion and a judgment?"], [49.24, "Opinions and judgments are fine, but it's important to recognize when they are opinions and judgments and to recognize the difference between those and objective facts like reports."], [61.68, "In doing so, we have better clarity about how it is that we think and how we lead our lives."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-6os0Xc5M2fQ", "title": "The World Isn't DIVIDED | Thought For the Day #41", "short_title": "The World Isn't DIVIDED", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 41, "blurb": "The truth of the matter is it's important for us to be able to look at the world in various perspectives and disciplines to the degree that we can.", "date": "2026-03-09", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/016-6os0Xc5M2fQ.mp3", "duration": 91, "audio_local": "audio/016-6os0Xc5M2fQ.mp3", "transcript": "Although we tend to split the world up in categories and we talk about psychology and philosophy and science and history, the world itself isn't divided, it just is. And in order for us to kind of navigate it, we split it into these categories. But the truth of the matter is, it's important for us to be able to look at the world in various perspectives and disciplines to the degree that we can. So we should read philosophy and read psychology and read science and read history and read different perspectives in these disciplines, because it allows for us to be able to see reality through various lenses. We can see the world from different eyes and different perspectives. That gives us a much broader and more developed way of understanding the nature of the world. And this is a line that is mentioned in the גמרא <i>Gemara</i>, מכל מלמדי השכלתי <i>Mikol melamdai hiskalti</i>. One of the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> says, I learn from all of my teachers. I learn from all those who have something to teach me. And it helps us in doing so to be able to see the world filled in in more vivid color and sharper image than simply by looking at it from one lens or one perspective.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Although we tend to split the world up in categories and we talk about psychology and philosophy and science and history, the world itself isn't divided, it just is."], [12.3, "And in order for us to kind of navigate it, we split it into these categories."], [16.46, "But the truth of the matter is, it's important for us to be able to look at the world in various perspectives and disciplines to the degree that we can."], [24.3, "So we should read philosophy and read psychology and read science and read history and read different perspectives in these disciplines, because it allows for us to be able to see reality through various lenses."], [46.3, "We can see the world from different eyes and different perspectives."], [51.22, "That gives us a much broader and more developed way of understanding the nature of the world."], [58.7, "And this is a line that is mentioned in the גמרא <i>Gemara</i>, מכל מלמדי השכלתי <i>Mikol melamdai hiskalti</i>."], [63.36, "One of the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> says, I learn from all of my teachers."], [66.84, "I learn from all those who have something to teach me."], [70.48, "And it helps us in doing so to be able to see the world filled in in more vivid color and sharper image than simply by looking at it from one lens or one perspective."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-U0tk64qP300", "title": "Different Types of TRUTH | Thought For the Day #40", "short_title": "Different Types of TRUTH", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 40, "blurb": "If an alien were to come in and you showed them Kane and Abel by Jeffrey Archer, and you showed them Hamlet by Shakespeare, what would they say?", "date": "2026-03-08", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/017-U0tk64qP300.mp3", "duration": 111, "audio_local": "audio/017-U0tk64qP300.mp3", "transcript": "Some things are true objectively. So the fact that the Earth is the third planet from the sun is objective fact. It doesn't matter whether you're human or alien, that is going to be true. But there are other truths that are anthropomorphic truths. They are truths that emerge from the human condition. Things that we develop as a result of our interactions of living with each other and what we tend to recognize as being beautiful or valuable. So what a human being sees as beautiful is unique to the human being. It's how we've developed, it's how we have grown to be able to see our world. So for me to say that Shakespeare is a better writer than Jeffrey Archer, in most human circles we would look and say of course Shakespeare is a better writer than Jeffrey Archer. He was a master wordsmith. There was nobody like him, there were very few of his caliber. But if an alien were to come in and you showed them Kane and Abel by Jeffrey Archer and you showed them Hamlet by Shakespeare, they may fail to see what exactly was the difference between the two outside of some kind of ratios of words. So the fact that there are truths that are anthropomorphically true because of our experience and understanding makes them no less valuable. As a matter of fact the תורה <i>Torah</i> calls that דרך ארץ <i>Derech Eretz</i> and it even says דרך ארץ קדמה לתורה <i>Derech Eretz kadma l'Torah</i>. דרך ארץ <i>Derech Eretz</i> is the foundational element upon which the תורה <i>Torah</i> speaks to us. So these are real elements of society, real elements of the world even though they are not as objective as the Earth being the third planet from the sun.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Some things are true objectively."], [2.8, "So the fact that the Earth is the third planet from the sun is objective fact."], [7.32, "It doesn't matter whether you're human or alien, that is going to be true."], [12.06, "But there are other truths that are anthropomorphic truths."], [15.42, "They are truths that emerge from the human condition."], [19.32, "Things that we develop as a result of our interactions of living with each other and what we tend to recognize as being beautiful or valuable."], [28.2, "So what a human being sees as beautiful is unique to the human being."], [32.7, "It's how we've developed, it's how we have grown to be able to see our world."], [37.44, "So for me to say that Shakespeare is a better writer than Jeffrey Archer, in most human circles we would look and say of course Shakespeare is a better writer than Jeffrey Archer."], [48.52, "He was a master wordsmith."], [50.96, "There was nobody like him, there were very few of his caliber."], [55.64, "But if an alien were to come in and you showed them Kane and Abel by Jeffrey Archer and you showed them Hamlet by Shakespeare, they may fail to see what exactly was the difference between the two outside of some kind of ratios of words."], [69.86, "So the fact that there are truths that are anthropomorphically true because of our experience and understanding makes them no less valuable."], [78.78, "As a matter of fact the תורה <i>Torah</i> calls that דרך ארץ <i>Derech Eretz</i> and it even says דרך ארץ קדמה לתורה <i>Derech Eretz kadma l'Torah</i>."], [84.9, "דרך ארץ <i>Derech Eretz</i> is the foundational element upon which the תורה <i>Torah</i> speaks to us."], [89.22, "So these are real elements of society, real elements of the world even though they are not as objective as the Earth being the third planet from the sun."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-4gZCHl6-IJ4", "title": "Can You CHOOSE to be INTELLIGENT? | Thought For the Day #39", "short_title": "Can You CHOOSE to be INTELLIGENT?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 39, "blurb": "What does Maimonides have to say?", "date": "2026-03-07", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/018-4gZCHl6-IJ4.mp3", "duration": 80, "audio_local": "audio/018-4gZCHl6-IJ4.mp3", "transcript": "Maimonides, the רמב״ם <i>Rambam</i>, famously talks about the variation of attributes that people have and that the right thing to do is to try and have a balanced middle line in terms of how it is that we act with regards to our character and attributes in the world: not to be too angry, not to be too sad, not to be too excited, not to be too happy. Try and find balance. But he says we can choose these things because the brain is quite plastic and we can train ourselves in order to be able to act in these ways. And one of the things that he says we can train ourselves to is intelligence. He says you can choose to be ignorant or you can choose to be intelligent, and the reality is that we can choose to be intelligent. We can educate ourselves and we live in a world today in where the ability to educate ourselves is more accessible than any time in history. We can choose to be more intelligent and there is an importance in being able to do this. We can't simply say, \\\"Oh, I don't know, I just never learned.\\\" You can learn. Do learn. Spend time learning something more today than you did yesterday. Choose something that you want to find more about and do it. Educate yourself. Raise your intelligence.", "sentences": [[0.56, "Maimonides, the רמב״ם <i>Rambam</i>, famously talks about the variation of attributes that people have and that the right thing to do is to try and have a balanced middle line in terms of how it is that we act with regards to our character and attributes in the world: not to be too angry, not to be too sad, not to be too excited, not to be too happy."], [18.14, "Try and find balance."], [19.5, "But he says we can choose these things because the brain is quite plastic and we can train ourselves in order to be able to act in these ways."], [26.5, "And one of the things that he says we can train ourselves to is intelligence."], [30.22, "He says you can choose to be ignorant or you can choose to be intelligent, and the reality is that we can choose to be intelligent."], [37.34, "We can educate ourselves and we live in a world today in where the ability to educate ourselves is more accessible than any time in history."], [45.9, "We can choose to be more intelligent and there is an importance in being able to do this."], [52.3, "We can't simply say, \\\"Oh, I don't know, I just never learned.\\\" You can learn."], [56.62, "Do learn."], [57.82, "Spend time learning something more today than you did yesterday."], [62.24, "Choose something that you want to find more about and do it."], [66.38, "Educate yourself."], [67.72, "Raise your intelligence."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-nZk3AZ4grrQ", "title": "When Did You Last THANK God With Tehillim? | Thought For the Day #38", "short_title": "When Did You Last THANK God With Tehillim?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 38, "blurb": "As much as we look to God to help us and indeed we should,\n\nand Tehillim is the one place that we should go to.\n\nIt's also the one place that we should go to,\n\nto give thanks and gratitude.", "date": "2026-03-01", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/019-nZk3AZ4grrQ.mp3", "duration": 45, "audio_local": "audio/019-nZk3AZ4grrQ.mp3", "transcript": "Often times we read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> when things are not going well in our life, and so we think, okay, there's something wrong or I have to worry about something happening, I better read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>. But what about reading תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> when things are going right in our lives? What about when things are wonderful for us, or when something goes really well? How often do we take תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> and sit down and thank God through its words? Acknowledge God, praise God through its words. As much as we look to God to help us, and indeed we should, and that is the one place that we should go to, it's also the one place that we should go to to give thanks and gratitude.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Often times we read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> when things are not going well in our life, and so we think, okay, there's something wrong or I have to worry about something happening, I better read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>."], [9.42, "But what about reading תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> when things are going right in our lives?"], [13.5, "What about when things are wonderful for us, or when something goes really well?"], [17.88, "How often do we take תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> and sit down and thank God through its words?"], [22.94, "Acknowledge God, praise God through its words."], [26.18, "As much as we look to God to help us, and indeed we should, and that is the one place that we should go to, it's also the one place that we should go to to give thanks and gratitude."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-YKJ8bSa_USQ", "title": "Only Argue If You Do THIS | Thought For the Day #37", "short_title": "Only Argue If You Do THIS", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 37, "blurb": "Arguments for the sake of heaven have both person in the argument really listening to the other when the other is speaking.", "date": "2026-02-28", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/020-YKJ8bSa_USQ.mp3", "duration": 72, "audio_local": "audio/020-YKJ8bSa_USQ.mp3", "transcript": "The תורה <i>Torah</i> says that some arguments are לשם שמים <i>l'shem shamayim</i>, they are for the sake of heaven, and some arguments are שלא לשם שמים <i>shelo l'shem shamayim</i>, they are not for the sake of heaven. The ones that are for the sake of heaven are wonderful and we should engage in them, the ones that are not, we should avoid at all costs. What are some elements that we can look for to know which is which? Arguments for the sake of heaven have both person in the argument really listening to the other when the other is speaking, because if our goal is to discern truth, which essentially is what an argument for the sake of heaven is, we need to hear the points that the other is making. Maybe they have something that we are not considering that is true, and in order to be able to determine that, we have to listen carefully. The second thing that you can look for in a productive argument is that if we are wrong, we admit that we're wrong, and we recognize that the point that was made that is contrary to what it is that we're thinking is the right one. Our goal here is not to win the argument; it is to achieve truth. If those are not present, don't argue.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> says that some arguments are לשם שמים <i>l'shem shamayim</i>, they are for the sake of heaven, and some arguments are שלא לשם שמים <i>shelo l'shem shamayim</i>, they are not for the sake of heaven."], [6.68, "The ones that are for the sake of heaven are wonderful and we should engage in them, the ones that are not, we should avoid at all costs."], [14.36, "What are some elements that we can look for to know which is which?"], [18.48, "Arguments for the sake of heaven have both person in the argument really listening to the other when the other is speaking, because if our goal is to discern truth, which essentially is what an argument for the sake of heaven is, we need to hear the points that the other is making."], [33.5, "Maybe they have something that we are not considering that is true, and in order to be able to determine that, we have to listen carefully."], [40.9, "The second thing that you can look for in a productive argument is that if we are wrong, we admit that we're wrong, and we recognize that the point that was made that is contrary to what it is that we're thinking is the right one."], [53.94, "Our goal here is not to win the argument; it is to achieve truth."], [58.8, "If those are not present, don't argue."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-DbgtvCaB_fc", "title": "DON'T Stifle Your Emotions! | Thought For the Day #36", "short_title": "DON'T Stifle Your Emotions!", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 36, "blurb": "One of the things that we can learn from that is that King David recognized the importance of allowing the full range of human emotion.", "date": "2026-02-27", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/021-DbgtvCaB_fc.mp3", "duration": 64, "audio_local": "audio/021-DbgtvCaB_fc.mp3", "transcript": "One of the remarkable aspects of תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> of the Psalms is that when you read the chapters, the range of emotion that you find within its pages presents the gamut of the human experience. There are psalms that are sad, there are psalms that are joyful, there are psalms that are frightening, there are psalms of all kinds. One of the things that we can learn from that is that King David recognized the importance of allowing the full range of human emotion to manifest in our experience of life and in our conversations with God. When we stifle our emotions, we do not allow ourselves to live. We do not have to be overcome by them, as a matter of fact, תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> is one way to deal with them. Speak about your feelings and if you don't have the words, read תהלים <i>Tehillim</i>, speak to God about those particular feelings. There is a chapter of תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> for practically every feeling in the book. Recognize the value of our human emotions, welcome them, both in our own human lives and before God.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the remarkable aspects of תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> of the Psalms is that when you read the chapters, the range of emotion that you find within its pages presents the gamut of the human experience."], [13.24, "There are psalms that are sad, there are psalms that are joyful, there are psalms that are frightening, there are psalms of all kinds."], [19.9, "One of the things that we can learn from that is that King David recognized the importance of allowing the full range of human emotion to manifest in our experience of life and in our conversations with God."], [34.1, "When we stifle our emotions, we do not allow ourselves to live."], [38.22, "We do not have to be overcome by them, as a matter of fact, תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> is one way to deal with them."], [43.18, "Speak about your feelings and if you don't have the words, read תהלים <i>Tehillim</i>, speak to God about those particular feelings."], [50.58, "There is a chapter of תהלים <i>Tehillim</i> for practically every feeling in the book."], [53.88, "Recognize the value of our human emotions, welcome them, both in our own human lives and before God."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-7_Wx3VDgoZo", "title": "Trouble With JEALOUSY? | Thought For the Day #35", "short_title": "Trouble With JEALOUSY?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 35, "blurb": "The Hakhamim in the Talmud say that a person is only jealous of someone that they share some commonality with.\n\nWhen we see somebody and we feel jealousy,\n\nit's because we relate to them.", "date": "2026-02-26", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/022-7_Wx3VDgoZo.mp3", "duration": 64, "audio_local": "audio/022-7_Wx3VDgoZo.mp3", "transcript": "How do we deal with jealousy? Well, one of the things is to understand the wiring of jealousy. חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> in the תלמוד <i>Talmud</i> say that a person is only jealous of someone that they share some commonality with. When we see somebody and we feel jealousy, it's because we relate to them. We see ourselves in some way as them, and that what they have should be mine. No one in this world can be who I can be, and that is true of every single one of us. When we truly accept and understand that, we recognize that nobody could ever take away what is uniquely ours, and that what is ours we either have or we can achieve, but it is never what someone else has. When we see people achieve things that have nothing to do with us, Olympic athletes, well, we cheer them on. It's when we feel that we should be the other person that we have the difficulties with jealousy. There is absolutely no other person in this world like you.", "sentences": [[0.0, "How do we deal with jealousy?"], [2.06, "Well, one of the things is to understand the wiring of jealousy."], [5.02, "חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> in the תלמוד <i>Talmud</i> say that a person is only jealous of someone that they share some commonality with."], [12.0, "When we see somebody and we feel jealousy, it's because we relate to them."], [16.42, "We see ourselves in some way as them, and that what they have should be mine."], [22.72, "No one in this world can be who I can be, and that is true of every single one of us."], [29.74, "When we truly accept and understand that, we recognize that nobody could ever take away what is uniquely ours, and that what is ours we either have or we can achieve, but it is never what someone else has."], [42.7, "When we see people achieve things that have nothing to do with us, Olympic athletes, well, we cheer them on."], [47.5, "It's when we feel that we should be the other person that we have the difficulties with jealousy."], [53.5, "There is absolutely no other person in this world like you."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-2fhFfGTPnKA", "title": "Who is RICH? | Thought For the Day #34", "short_title": "Who is RICH?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 34, "blurb": "Be happy in who you are,\n\nwho you are as a creation.\n\nThat's your portion.\n\nAnd the simha, the happiness, there is not tolerance,\n\nit's not putting up with it, it's not accepting it.\n\nIt's being happy with it.\n\nIt's genuinely having joy for who you are in the world.", "date": "2026-02-25", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/023-2fhFfGTPnKA.mp3", "duration": 64, "audio_local": "audio/023-2fhFfGTPnKA.mp3", "transcript": "The חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> in the משנה <i>Mishnah</i> in אבות <i>Avot</i> famously say איזהו עשיר השמח בחלקו <i>Eizehu ashir? Hasameach bechelko.</i> Who's rich? A person who is happy with their portion. And most people understand the portion to be what I have. But that's not really what חלקו <i>chelko</i> means. What it means to be happy with your חלק <i>chelek</i> is to be happy in who you are. Who you are as a creation, that's your portion. And the שמחה <i>simcha</i>, the happiness there is not tolerance, it's not putting up with it, it's not accepting it. It's being happy with it. It's genuinely having joy for who you are in the world. And when you have that, whether you have possessions, more or less, you have money, more or less, that of course can enhance and restrict a person's experience in the world. But happiness in who one is is at the foundation of wealth in this world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> in the משנה <i>Mishnah</i> in אבות <i>Avot</i> famously say איזהו עשיר השמח בחלקו <i>Eizehu ashir?"], [3.86, "Hasameach bechelko.</i> Who's rich?"], [6.22, "A person who is happy with their portion."], [8.7, "And most people understand the portion to be what I have."], [12.4, "But that's not really what חלקו <i>chelko</i> means."], [15.1, "What it means to be happy with your חלק <i>chelek</i> is to be happy in who you are."], [20.34, "Who you are as a creation, that's your portion."], [23.12, "And the שמחה <i>simcha</i>, the happiness there is not tolerance, it's not putting up with it, it's not accepting it."], [28.22, "It's being happy with it."], [30.16, "It's genuinely having joy for who you are in the world."], [34.44, "And when you have that, whether you have possessions, more or less, you have money, more or less, that of course can enhance and restrict a person's experience in the world."], [44.68, "But happiness in who one is is at the foundation of wealth in this world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-sj_N3yXcXQM", "title": "What is a TZADIK? | Thought For the Day #33", "short_title": "What is a TZADIK?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 33, "blurb": "Tzedaka is the act of the tzadik, and tzedaka is not only charity.\n\nWhat it means is that\n\nwe look for what is wrong with the world\n\nand we do what we can to make it right.\n\nDoing tzedaka (oseh tzedaka) is looking for what is missing in the world.", "date": "2026-02-24", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/024-sj_N3yXcXQM.mp3", "duration": 73, "audio_local": "audio/024-sj_N3yXcXQM.mp3", "transcript": "What does it mean to be a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>? Well, one of the things simply that it means is that you are בצדק <i>be'tzedek</i>, you are in the right or righteous, which is all that righteous means. Righteous means that you are in the right and not in the wrong. In order to be a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>, it means that you have to perform צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>. צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is the act of the צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>, and צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is not only charity. What it means is that we look for what is wrong with the world and we do what we can to make it right. Doing צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>, עושה צדקה <i>Oseh Tzedakah</i>, is looking for what is missing in the world and filling it and doing what is right. And so of course that means if someone does not have enough money and you're able to help them, then you give them money in order to be able to help them, that is צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>. But it also means that you look for anything that might be wrong in the world. A person's mood is down, a person is feeling unwell, and you can do something in order to be able to make them feel better, that too is צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>. You're seeing something that is wrong in the world and you're doing what you can to make it right. That is the path of a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>.", "sentences": [[0.0, "What does it mean to be a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>?"], [2.68, "Well, one of the things simply that it means is that you are בצדק <i>be'tzedek</i>, you are in the right or righteous, which is all that righteous means."], [11.84, "Righteous means that you are in the right and not in the wrong."], [14.6, "In order to be a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>, it means that you have to perform צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>."], [19.32, "צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is the act of the צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>, and צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is not only charity."], [23.4, "What it means is that we look for what is wrong with the world and we do what we can to make it right."], [30.74, "Doing צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>, עושה צדקה <i>Oseh Tzedakah</i>, is looking for what is missing in the world and filling it and doing what is right."], [38.14, "And so of course that means if someone does not have enough money and you're able to help them, then you give them money in order to be able to help them, that is צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>."], [45.62, "But it also means that you look for anything that might be wrong in the world."], [49.34, "A person's mood is down, a person is feeling unwell, and you can do something in order to be able to make them feel better, that too is צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>."], [57.36, "You're seeing something that is wrong in the world and you're doing what you can to make it right."], [61.58, "That is the path of a צדיק <i>Tzadik</i>."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-IbMsJEahuJs", "title": "What We KNOW Isn't the Entire Picture | Thought For the Day #32", "short_title": "What We KNOW Isn't the Entire Picture", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 32, "blurb": "Our goal is never to be right. Our goal is to find truth.", "date": "2026-02-23", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/026-IbMsJEahuJs.mp3", "duration": 51, "audio_local": "audio/026-IbMsJEahuJs.mp3", "transcript": "There is a high ideal in תורה <i>Torah</i> to discover truth. Therefore, we have to be careful about what we already know. Maybe what we already know isn't the entire picture. Maybe it's wrong. And therefore, one way to think about our ideas is to consider whatever it is that we are holding as truth now as working definitions. This is what I know to be true now. This is how it is I'm living because it's all the information that I have. But if I find something that proves to me that I need to relinquish it, I relinquish it and I take on what is true. Our goal is never to be right. Our goal is to find truth.", "sentences": [[0.0, "There is a high ideal in תורה <i>Torah</i> to discover truth."], [4.12, "Therefore, we have to be careful about what we already know."], [7.76, "Maybe what we already know isn't the entire picture."], [10.96, "Maybe it's wrong."], [12.34, "And therefore, one way to think about our ideas is to consider whatever it is that we are holding as truth now as working definitions."], [22.16, "This is what I know to be true now."], [23.88, "This is how it is I'm living because it's all the information that I have."], [27.0, "But if I find something that proves to me that I need to relinquish it, I relinquish it and I take on what is true."], [35.36, "Our goal is never to be right."], [38.3, "Our goal is to find truth."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-A-CKD763qXY", "title": "DON'T Be a Fundamentalist | Thought For the Day #31", "short_title": "DON'T Be a Fundamentalist", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 31, "blurb": "There is a great deal that we do not know.\n\nEverything has questions that we yet do not have answers to.\n\nThe tradition of Torah and Judaism  moves away from fundamentalism in that regard. God has not disclosed every last thing to us and that even that which he has disclosed, we don't always understand. We're not always clear.\n\nThe pirit of Jewish thought is not certainty, it is questioning.", "date": "2026-02-21", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/027-A-CKD763qXY.mp3", "duration": 68, "audio_local": "audio/027-A-CKD763qXY.mp3", "transcript": "One of the reasons that people get into fundamentalist ideas and fundamentalist thinking is because fundamentalist thinking is black and white. You know the answers and there's very little doubt in fundamentalist thought when in reality is that the world is very much uncertain. There is a great deal that we do not know. Everything has questions that we yet do not have answers to. The tradition of תורה <i>Torah</i> and Judaism moves away from fundamentalism in that regard. God has not disclosed every last thing to us and that even that which he has disclosed we don't always understand. We're not always clear. Spirit of Jewish thought is not certainty. It is questioning. It is recognizing that we are a people that prefer questions that cannot be answered to answers that cannot be questioned. That is our tradition and that is what we should commit to.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the reasons that people get into fundamentalist ideas and fundamentalist thinking is because fundamentalist thinking is black and white."], [9.4, "You know the answers and there's very little doubt in fundamentalist thought when in reality is that the world is very much uncertain."], [19.5, "There is a great deal that we do not know."], [23.0, "Everything has questions that we yet do not have answers to."], [27.5, "The tradition of תורה <i>Torah</i> and Judaism moves away from fundamentalism in that regard."], [32.3, "God has not disclosed every last thing to us and that even that which he has disclosed we don't always understand."], [39.88, "We're not always clear."], [41.26, "Spirit of Jewish thought is not certainty."], [45.28, "It is questioning."], [46.68, "It is recognizing that we are a people that prefer questions that cannot be answered to answers that cannot be questioned."], [54.8, "That is our tradition and that is what we should commit to."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-n3hKXB1toM0", "title": "It is a MITZVA to Be Healthy  | Thought For the Day #29", "short_title": "It is a MITZVA to Be Healthy", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 29, "blurb": "To be healthy, both in mind and in body, is not just a good idea.\n\nIt's not just a better way to live.\n\nAs far as Torah is concerned,\n\nit is a mitzva to do so.", "date": "2026-02-19", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/028-n3hKXB1toM0.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/028-n3hKXB1toM0.mp3", "transcript": "Involved in the מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> of knowing God is the ability to be able to get to the knowledge. And so הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>, Maimonides, writes a great deal about physical health in his הלכות <i>Halachot</i>, and he talks about what a good diet looks like, how exercise should come into the fray. This is not just advice from הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>. הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i> is writing this as הלכה <i>Halacha</i>. That means that to be healthy both in mind and in body is not just a good idea, it's not just a better way to live. As far as תורה <i>Torah</i> is concerned, it is a מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> to do so. That means to keep ourselves healthy, to keep our mind healthy, to keep our body healthy, not just to respond to sickness when it comes, but to maintain health, is all part of the מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> of being well and healthy in order to come to know God.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Involved in the מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> of knowing God is the ability to be able to get to the knowledge."], [6.44, "And so הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>, Maimonides, writes a great deal about physical health in his הלכות <i>Halachot</i>, and he talks about what a good diet looks like, how exercise should come into the fray."], [19.38, "This is not just advice from הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i>."], [22.78, "הרמב\\\"ם <i>HaRambam</i> is writing this as הלכה <i>Halacha</i>."], [25.88, "That means that to be healthy both in mind and in body is not just a good idea, it's not just a better way to live."], [33.72, "As far as תורה <i>Torah</i> is concerned, it is a מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> to do so."], [37.76, "That means to keep ourselves healthy, to keep our mind healthy, to keep our body healthy, not just to respond to sickness when it comes, but to maintain health, is all part of the מצוה <i>Mitzvah</i> of being well and healthy in order to come to know God."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-898UPUOGLCI", "title": "An INSIGHT From King David | Thought For the Day #28 #history #wisdom #power", "short_title": "An INSIGHT From King David", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 28, "blurb": "If we look at King David, he does not say: \n\n\"Where is God? God doesn't talk to me.\"\n\nHe says:\n\n\"I have placed God before me. Always. And although God is not an entity that we can put somewhere.\"", "date": "2026-02-18", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/029-898UPUOGLCI.mp3", "duration": 68, "audio_local": "audio/029-898UPUOGLCI.mp3", "transcript": "King David wrote in תהלים <i>Tehillim</i>: שויתי ה' לנגדי תמיד <i>Shiviti Adonai Lenegdi Tamid</i>. I have placed God before me, always. And people often say, \\\"Where is God? I don't feel God, I don't see God, God doesn't talk to me.\\\" But if we look at King David, what he does is not say \\\"Where is God? God doesn't talk to me.\\\" He says, \\\"I have placed God before me, always.\\\" And although God is not an entity that we can put somewhere, the question is more: how do we create space in our lives? How do we create the conditions in our lives in which we welcome God? In which we actually wish for Him to be before us always? This world is not a world in which God asserts His existence. It is a world in which the conditions for His presence must be created by us. And the question for us is: what do we need to do in order to be able to create that space?", "sentences": [[0.0, "King David wrote in תהלים <i>Tehillim</i>: שויתי ה' לנגדי תמיד <i>Shiviti Adonai Lenegdi Tamid</i>."], [4.16, "I have placed God before me, always."], [7.32, "And people often say, \\\"Where is God?"], [10.18, "I don't feel God, I don't see God, God doesn't talk to me.\\\" But if we look at King David, what he does is not say \\\"Where is God?"], [19.0, "God doesn't talk to me.\\\" He says, \\\"I have placed God before me, always.\\\" And although God is not an entity that we can put somewhere, the question is more: how do we create space in our lives?"], [31.9, "How do we create the conditions in our lives in which we welcome God?"], [36.06, "In which we actually wish for Him to be before us always?"], [41.88, "This world is not a world in which God asserts His existence."], [45.52, "It is a world in which the conditions for His presence must be created by us."], [51.02, "And the question for us is: what do we need to do in order to be able to create that space?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-2QO0uHExOnA", "title": "How Can We UNDERSTAND God? | Thought For the Day #27", "short_title": "How Can We UNDERSTAND God?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 27, "blurb": "When we better understand God's world, we better understand God...", "date": "2026-02-17", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/030-2QO0uHExOnA.mp3", "duration": 73, "audio_local": "audio/030-2QO0uHExOnA.mp3", "transcript": "Often times people say if we are interested in studying Judaism and we want to study תורה <i>Torah</i> and we want to be able to become better Jews that there is no real need to study anything else in the world. And sometimes concessions are made, science, understandable, mathematics, understandable, but Shakespeare, literature, arts, psychology, philosophy, those things, not so much. What's important to recognize is that what we are doing when we study anything that is essentially not תורה <i>Torah</i> proper is to that helps us to understand the world, is to better come to understand God. And Shakespeare and Rembrandt and Freud were all creations of God. And to understand their minds, to be able to have a sense of what it was their perspective was on things and some of the beauty and intelligence that they brought out into the world is to better understand God's world and when we better understand God's world we better understand God.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Often times people say if we are interested in studying Judaism and we want to study תורה <i>Torah</i> and we want to be able to become better Jews that there is no real need to study anything else in the world."], [12.08, "And sometimes concessions are made, science, understandable, mathematics, understandable, but Shakespeare, literature, arts, psychology, philosophy, those things, not so much."], [25.72, "What's important to recognize is that what we are doing when we study anything that is essentially not תורה <i>Torah</i> proper is to that helps us to understand the world, is to better come to understand God."], [38.6, "And Shakespeare and Rembrandt and Freud were all creations of God."], [46.32, "And to understand their minds, to be able to have a sense of what it was their perspective was on things and some of the beauty and intelligence that they brought out into the world is to better understand God's world and when we better understand God's world we better understand God."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-7leQIol7fm4", "title": "What Do You CONTROL? | Thought For the Day #26", "short_title": "What Do You CONTROL?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 26, "blurb": "Have some clarity around what it is that you can control and what it is that you can't, what you can control, put your whole self into doing and what you can't control, give to Him...", "date": "2026-02-16", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/032-7leQIol7fm4.mp3", "duration": 55, "audio_local": "audio/032-7leQIol7fm4.mp3", "transcript": "How do we know what it is that we're supposed to do in our lives and what it is that we're supposed to give to God and have trust in Him for doing? We can ask ourselves one basic question: What in this situation can I control and what can I not control? And having clarity around that question really answers that doubt. There are things in life that we have the ability to control that we sometimes think we can't, and there are things in life that we do not have the ability to control that we believe we can. To have clarity around that answers the question. So in a situation in which you might not be clear, what is אמונה <i>emunah</i> in God and where should I, should I stop my involvement in an issue and give it over to Him? Have some clarity around what it is you can control and what it is you can't. What you can control, put your whole self into doing and what you can't control, give to השם <i>Hashem</i>.", "sentences": [[0.0, "How do we know what it is that we're supposed to do in our lives and what it is that we're supposed to give to God and have trust in Him for doing?"], [8.66, "We can ask ourselves one basic question: What in this situation can I control and what can I not control?"], [16.76, "And having clarity around that question really answers that doubt."], [21.56, "There are things in life that we have the ability to control that we sometimes think we can't, and there are things in life that we do not have the ability to control that we believe we can."], [30.56, "To have clarity around that answers the question."], [33.88, "So in a situation in which you might not be clear, what is אמונה <i>emunah</i> in God and where should I, should I stop my involvement in an issue and give it over to Him?"], [43.14, "Have some clarity around what it is you can control and what it is you can't."], [47.5, "What you can control, put your whole self into doing and what you can't control, give to השם <i>Hashem</i>."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-OmRVs2WbkpA", "title": "How Should We SPEAK? | Thought For the Day #25", "short_title": "How Should We SPEAK?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 25, "blurb": "It’s not just important what we say, but also how we say it and the slant we put on it…", "date": "2026-02-15", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/033-OmRVs2WbkpA.mp3", "duration": 68, "audio_local": "audio/033-OmRVs2WbkpA.mp3", "transcript": "לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i>, speaking negatively is a major prohibition of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the תורה <i>Torah</i> cautions us very strongly against doing that. But oftentimes we think that לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is problematic because it's false. לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is not problematic because it's false. לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is not problematic because it's false, it's problematic because it's true, but the way that we say things are negatively slanted, that we make people or encourage people think about things negatively. One thing that we can think next time that we're presented with engaging in this kind of dialogue is what would it be like if the entire society only spoke this way, and how will my contribution in framing things negatively or slanting them negatively create the realities of the society that we live in? תורה <i>Torah</i> looks for us to live in a nourishing, whole, healthy, robust society. And when we speak negatively about truths, when we frame them in ways that people hear them in poor or bad terms, it affects the realities in which we live. And so if we don't have to say something negative, it's better not to, and if you can slant something in a positive, it's always good to try.", "sentences": [[0.0, "לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i>, speaking negatively is a major prohibition of the תורה <i>Torah</i>, the תורה <i>Torah</i> cautions us very strongly against doing that."], [7.78, "But oftentimes we think that לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is problematic because it's false."], [13.0, "לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is not problematic because it's false."], [15.3, "לשון הרע <i>Lashon hara</i> is not problematic because it's false, it's problematic because it's true, but the way that we say things are negatively slanted, that we make people or encourage people think about things negatively."], [23.34, "One thing that we can think next time that we're presented with engaging in this kind of dialogue is what would it be like if the entire society only spoke this way, and how will my contribution in framing things negatively or slanting them negatively create the realities of the society that we live in?"], [42.68, "תורה <i>Torah</i> looks for us to live in a nourishing, whole, healthy, robust society."], [45.66, "And when we speak negatively about truths, when we frame them in ways that people hear them in poor or bad terms, it affects the realities in which we live."], [54.64, "And so if we don't have to say something negative, it's better not to, and if you can slant something in a positive, it's always good to try."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd--Bd8gOtXI9g", "title": "Take Another SHOT | Thought For the Day #24", "short_title": "Take Another SHOT", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 24, "blurb": "Even if you miss the mark, try again...", "date": "2026-02-13", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/034--Bd8gOtXI9g.mp3", "duration": 66, "audio_local": "audio/034--Bd8gOtXI9g.mp3", "transcript": "The word for sin in Hebrew is חטא <i>chet</i>, and חטא <i>chet</i> literally means missing the mark. And one of the things that is important about how it is that we live our lives, especially when we talk about חטא <i>chet</i> or talk about sin, is that oftentimes when we do things that are wrong or we misstep, we get down on ourselves. And because of that, we just stop trying. But it's important to recognize that the point of missing the mark is not to just drop the arrow or the bow and arrow, so to speak, and stop trying to aim for the target. You keep aiming for the target. If you missed it once, take another shot. If you missed it twice, take another shot. The point is to constantly reengage in living our lives in the best possible way so that we actually do, through practice, hit the target. The worst thing that a person can do when they misstep or make a wrong move in life is to stop living, is to stop trying, is to give up. Never give up. Always take another shot, and eventually, you make the mark.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The word for sin in Hebrew is חטא <i>chet</i>, and חטא <i>chet</i> literally means missing the mark."], [5.66, "And one of the things that is important about how it is that we live our lives, especially when we talk about חטא <i>chet</i> or talk about sin, is that oftentimes when we do things that are wrong or we misstep, we get down on ourselves."], [19.7, "And because of that, we just stop trying."], [22.3, "But it's important to recognize that the point of missing the mark is not to just drop the arrow or the bow and arrow, so to speak, and stop trying to aim for the target."], [32.4, "You keep aiming for the target."], [34.2, "If you missed it once, take another shot."], [36.68, "If you missed it twice, take another shot."], [38.78, "The point is to constantly reengage in living our lives in the best possible way so that we actually do, through practice, hit the target."], [48.36, "The worst thing that a person can do when they misstep or make a wrong move in life is to stop living, is to stop trying, is to give up."], [56.98, "Never give up."], [58.02, "Always take another shot, and eventually, you make the mark."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-KdvMvkM7pIY", "title": "How PRECIOUS is Your Life? | Thought For the Day #23", "short_title": "How PRECIOUS is Your Life?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 23, "blurb": "Just as we carefully measure gold, we should carefully measure our lives...", "date": "2026-02-12", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/035-KdvMvkM7pIY.mp3", "duration": 74, "audio_local": "audio/035-KdvMvkM7pIY.mp3", "transcript": "רבי משה חיים לוצאטו <i>Rabbi Moshe Chaim Luzzatto</i> in the מסילת ישרים <i>Mesillat Yesharim</i> writes a beautiful point. He says when we really care about things, we measure them very carefully. So he gives an example of a person measuring gold. You know, we measure gold in the most minute weights and most minute measures because it's so precious. We want to make sure that we have it accurately. And he says shouldn't our lives be at least as precious as gold? I mean it's the most important thing that we do, it's all that we have. And so he says that it is worth taking time to measure our lives, to evaluate how it is that we are living and how we're doing on a regular basis. If we don't take the time consciously to do that, we end up waking up to a life that isn't really lived consciously, it's essentially lived on autopilot. It's a wise thing to do to take some time, if not every day, maybe once a week, to think about how it is that your life is going. How are your relationships? How is your self-care and self-worth? How is your work and productivity? In what nature and in what frame are you living and can it be changed for the better? If we do that, we genuinely treat our lives as precious.", "sentences": [[0.0, "רבי משה חיים לוצאטו <i>Rabbi Moshe Chaim Luzzatto</i> in the מסילת ישרים <i>Mesillat Yesharim</i> writes a beautiful point."], [3.7, "He says when we really care about things, we measure them very carefully."], [8.6, "So he gives an example of a person measuring gold."], [10.94, "You know, we measure gold in the most minute weights and most minute measures because it's so precious."], [16.38, "We want to make sure that we have it accurately."], [18.88, "And he says shouldn't our lives be at least as precious as gold?"], [23.22, "I mean it's the most important thing that we do, it's all that we have."], [26.9, "And so he says that it is worth taking time to measure our lives, to evaluate how it is that we are living and how we're doing on a regular basis."], [35.82, "If we don't take the time consciously to do that, we end up waking up to a life that isn't really lived consciously, it's essentially lived on autopilot."], [45.48, "It's a wise thing to do to take some time, if not every day, maybe once a week, to think about how it is that your life is going."], [53.4, "How are your relationships?"], [54.74, "How is your self-care and self-worth?"], [57.0, "How is your work and productivity?"], [59.34, "In what nature and in what frame are you living and can it be changed for the better?"], [64.5, "If we do that, we genuinely treat our lives as precious."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-h6mzOdrJvu8", "title": "How to RETURN to Yourself | Thought For the Day #22", "short_title": "How to RETURN to Yourself", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 22, "blurb": "To return to God is to realize and recognize the creation that we are.", "date": "2026-02-11", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/036-h6mzOdrJvu8.mp3", "duration": 54, "audio_local": "audio/036-h6mzOdrJvu8.mp3", "transcript": "What is תשובה <i>Teshuvah</i>, repentance, literally return? הרב קוק <i>Rav Kook</i> writes a remarkable point and says that the first step of תשובה <i>Teshuvah</i>, of return, is the return to ourselves. That there are elements of ourselves that we shun, that we quiet, that we put into the dark spaces and don't pay attention to because we might feel that they are inadequate, or that they are not good, or that people have told us that they don't matter, or that they aren't fitting. But it is important for us to recognize God created us, the entirety of who we are. And to return to Him is first to acknowledge the creation that we are, that is the full work of His hands. And when we do so, says הרב קוק <i>Rav Kook</i>, we immediately lean into the hands of God.", "sentences": [[0.0, "What is תשובה <i>Teshuvah</i>, repentance, literally return?"], [5.64, "הרב קוק <i>Rav Kook</i> writes a remarkable point and says that the first step of תשובה <i>Teshuvah</i>, of return, is the return to ourselves."], [13.94, "That there are elements of ourselves that we shun, that we quiet, that we put into the dark spaces and don't pay attention to because we might feel that they are inadequate, or that they are not good, or that people have told us that they don't matter, or that they aren't fitting."], [31.54, "But it is important for us to recognize God created us, the entirety of who we are."], [38.24, "And to return to Him is first to acknowledge the creation that we are, that is the full work of His hands."], [46.36, "And when we do so, says הרב קוק <i>Rav Kook</i>, we immediately lean into the hands of God."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-jnfUMMmkBao", "title": "Do Your PARTS Know Eachother? | Thought For the Day #21", "short_title": "Do Your PARTS Know Eachother?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 21, "blurb": "We often wake up as one person and go to sleep as another. Between the morning and the night, we encounter different versions of ourselves—some we are proud of, and others we deem inappropriate, unsubstantial, or better left in the dark.\n\nBut the daily command to love with all your hearts (plural) suggests that \"righteousness\" isn't a search-and-destroy mission for your flaws. It’s an invitation to a conversation.", "date": "2026-02-10", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/037-jnfUMMmkBao.mp3", "duration": 85, "audio_local": "audio/037-jnfUMMmkBao.mp3", "transcript": "Most of us, if not all of us, have different parts of ourselves. We will say in our everyday language, part of me feels this and part of me thinks this, and oftentimes those parts can be in conflict. We feel very strongly or are interested in doing something at the beginning of the week and by the end of the week we're not interested in it anymore. Sometimes that can happen between morning and night. The question is what do we do with those parts? There is a פסוק <i>Pasuk</i> that we say every day that we are to love God בכל לבבך <i>bechol levavecha</i> with all of your hearts, which is plural. And the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say it should just say בכל לבך <i>bechol libcha</i> with your heart. Why does it have heart in plural? And the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say, well, there's different parts of us. And there is an encouragement in the תורה <i>Torah</i> not to kill off the parts that you deem to be unsubstantial or inappropriate in serving God, but rather to recognize them, look at them, and to integrate them into a whole self. Through the entirety that God created, serve and ultimately seek righteousness. And so a good question that we might ask ourselves is do our parts know each other? Do they communicate with each other? Is there a way for me to bring even the parts that might be in the shadows into the light and to encourage a whole self and a whole involvement in our own growth and development as people and souls?", "sentences": [[0.0, "Most of us, if not all of us, have different parts of ourselves."], [5.24, "We will say in our everyday language, part of me feels this and part of me thinks this, and oftentimes those parts can be in conflict."], [13.58, "We feel very strongly or are interested in doing something at the beginning of the week and by the end of the week we're not interested in it anymore."], [20.08, "Sometimes that can happen between morning and night."], [22.44, "The question is what do we do with those parts?"], [24.82, "There is a פסוק <i>Pasuk</i> that we say every day that we are to love God בכל לבבך <i>bechol levavecha</i> with all of your hearts, which is plural."], [32.28, "And the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say it should just say בכל לבך <i>bechol libcha</i> with your heart."], [35.56, "Why does it have heart in plural?"], [37.04, "And the חכמים <i>Chachamim</i> say, well, there's different parts of us."], [39.56, "And there is an encouragement in the תורה <i>Torah</i> not to kill off the parts that you deem to be unsubstantial or inappropriate in serving God, but rather to recognize them, look at them, and to integrate them into a whole self."], [56.16, "Through the entirety that God created, serve and ultimately seek righteousness."], [62.8, "And so a good question that we might ask ourselves is do our parts know each other?"], [67.72, "Do they communicate with each other?"], [69.58, "Is there a way for me to bring even the parts that might be in the shadows into the light and to encourage a whole self and a whole involvement in our own growth and development as people and souls?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-fnbunP-h1yc", "title": "Who's REALLY in Charge? | Thought For the Day #20", "short_title": "Who's REALLY in Charge?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 20, "blurb": "Is the world a trap or a tool? According to the Torah and the Mesillat Yesharim, the world isn't here to distract you—it’s here to support your growth. \n\nThe real question is: Who is driving? Are you using the world to serve a higher purpose, or is the world using you?", "date": "2026-02-09", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/038-fnbunP-h1yc.mp3", "duration": 61, "audio_local": "audio/038-fnbunP-h1yc.mp3", "transcript": "תורה <i>Torah</i> does not present the world to us as a major trap or distraction or a way of enticing us away from what's important and what matters. To the contrary, the רמח״ל <i>Ramchal</i> writes in the מסילת ישרים <i>Mesillat Yesharim</i>, for example, that the world is here to support us in our growth and development and connection to God. And a question that's important for us to ask or to recognize is who's driving whom? Are we using the world as a means to grow and develop and therefore we are the drivers, or are we being driven by it? Are we being driven by our desires, our inclinations, our whims, our feelings? So when we eat or when we sleep or when we do anything that we do in the world, we can do those things as service, as part of the way that we serve God and ultimately grow ourselves. An important question that we can ask ourselves is in any situation, am I using this or is it using me?", "sentences": [[0.0, "תורה <i>Torah</i> does not present the world to us as a major trap or distraction or a way of enticing us away from what's important and what matters."], [9.32, "To the contrary, the רמח״ל <i>Ramchal</i> writes in the מסילת ישרים <i>Mesillat Yesharim</i>, for example, that the world is here to support us in our growth and development and connection to God."], [18.8, "And a question that's important for us to ask or to recognize is who's driving whom?"], [24.12, "Are we using the world as a means to grow and develop and therefore we are the drivers, or are we being driven by it?"], [33.16, "Are we being driven by our desires, our inclinations, our whims, our feelings?"], [39.82, "So when we eat or when we sleep or when we do anything that we do in the world, we can do those things as service, as part of the way that we serve God and ultimately grow ourselves."], [52.04, "An important question that we can ask ourselves is in any situation, am I using this or is it using me?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-jhs8gPwhHI0", "title": "Things Matter MORE | Thought For the Day #19", "short_title": "Things Matter MORE", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 19, "blurb": "In a casual friendship, a missed text is no big deal. In a deep romance, it’s everything.\n\nLoving relationships are high-stakes. When we love someone deeply, the \"details\" aren't just chores—they are expressions of care. This is the heart of Halakha. It isn't just about the law; it’s about the specificity, attention, and care we bring to our relationship with the Divine.", "date": "2026-02-08", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/039-jhs8gPwhHI0.mp3", "duration": 67, "audio_local": "audio/039-jhs8gPwhHI0.mp3", "transcript": "When we're in loving relationships, things matter more, not less. Small things that otherwise would be insignificant in casual encounters become essential elements of a relationship. A simple good morning or a good bye, a phone call or a text message. Avoiding those things or missing those things in a relationship of love matters more than simple acquaintances. And because of that we recognize that details matter in a relationship of high stakes because loving relationships are high stakes relationships. And because of that in our relationship with God, things like הלכה <i>Halakha</i> matter, the law matters, the specifics of how it is we do things, the care that we have in terms of how it is that we do it makes a very big difference. It is never just about the action, it's always about the specificity, attention and care in the action and what הלכה <i>Halakha</i> does is help us to orient ourselves appropriately to be careful about the details that we need to pay attention to in the loving relationship that we have.", "sentences": [[0.0, "When we're in loving relationships, things matter more, not less."], [5.28, "Small things that otherwise would be insignificant in casual encounters become essential elements of a relationship."], [13.46, "A simple good morning or a good bye, a phone call or a text message."], [17.84, "Avoiding those things or missing those things in a relationship of love matters more than simple acquaintances."], [23.96, "And because of that we recognize that details matter in a relationship of high stakes because loving relationships are high stakes relationships."], [33.9, "And because of that in our relationship with God, things like הלכה <i>Halakha</i> matter, the law matters, the specifics of how it is we do things, the care that we have in terms of how it is that we do it makes a very big difference."], [47.54, "It is never just about the action, it's always about the specificity, attention and care in the action and what הלכה <i>Halakha</i> does is help us to orient ourselves appropriately to be careful about the details that we need to pay attention to in the loving relationship that we have."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-D4W6wF89Wgw", "title": "Why Pray? | Thought For the Day #18", "short_title": "Why Pray?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 18, "blurb": "If God is all-knowing, prayer might seem redundant—unless you view it as a partnership. When we share our struggles and ask for help, we aren't informing God; we are inviting Him into the process.", "date": "2026-02-07", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/040-D4W6wF89Wgw.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/040-D4W6wF89Wgw.mp3", "transcript": "When we pray, we're not informing God about what's going on in our lives. I mean, he's God. He knows what's going on in our lives. Then what's the purpose of prayer? One of the things that prayer does is it engages in the relationship. It is a joint endeavor that you are doing in living your life together with God. And as his partner, you're sharing with your partner what it is that's going on for you. Not because he doesn't know, but because the sharing itself is valuable in the relationship. And in that sharing, you ask, \\\"This is really a difficulty for me. This particular issue is I'm struggling with. Do you think you can help me?\\\" And the very act of opening up to God in that regard connects us to him and also opens the channels for him to come into our lives. When we pray, we connect with God. We acknowledge the joint endeavor of the", "sentences": [[0.6, "When we pray, we're not informing God about what's going on in our lives."], [5.26, "I mean, he's God."], [6.74, "He knows what's going on in our lives."], [8.48, "Then what's the purpose of prayer?"], [11.08, "One of the things that prayer does is it engages in the relationship."], [15.84, "It is a joint endeavor that you are doing in living your life together with God."], [21.48, "And as his partner, you're sharing with your partner what it is that's going on for you."], [27.0, "Not because he doesn't know, but because the sharing itself is valuable in the relationship."], [32.48, "And in that sharing, you ask, \\\"This is really a difficulty for me."], [36.64, "This particular issue is I'm struggling with."], [40.44, "Do you think you can help me?\\\" And the very act of opening up to God in that regard connects us to him and also opens the channels for him to come into our lives."], [54.5, "When we pray, we connect with God."], [58.04, "We acknowledge the joint endeavor of the"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-KA8db-ZpAIM", "title": "EVERYTHING is Service | Thought For the Day #17", "short_title": "EVERYTHING is Service", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 17, "blurb": "Your entire life can be a service to God. It’s not just about prayer—it’s about the reason behind your rest, your food, and your movement. If your aim is connection, every action becomes divine.", "date": "2026-02-06", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/041-KA8db-ZpAIM.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/041-KA8db-ZpAIM.mp3", "transcript": "The רמב\\\"ם <i>Rambam</i> presents a radical idea and says that practically everything in our life can be עבודה <i>Avodah</i>, can be service to God. The only question to ask is why. The why makes the difference. What is the reason I am doing it? What is the motivating factor? So, it could be that I eat or sleep or exercise or play, socialize, and that can be עבודה <i>Avodah</i>, it can be service, if the answer to the why is: because it helps me, it gives me strength, it brings me mental clarity in order to be able to connect to God. And if that is our aim and frame, that is service. The important thing to ask ourselves, more even than what am I doing, is why am I doing it? And what is the highest and most valuable reason for doing so? When it is to serve and connect to our best selves and ultimately connect to God, that itself is.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The רמב\\\"ם <i>Rambam</i> presents a radical idea and says that practically everything in our life can be עבודה <i>Avodah</i>, can be service to God."], [10.2, "The only question to ask is why."], [13.8, "The why makes the difference."], [15.8, "What is the reason I am doing it?"], [17.82, "What is the motivating factor?"], [19.36, "So, it could be that I eat or sleep or exercise or play, socialize, and that can be עבודה <i>Avodah</i>, it can be service, if the answer to the why is: because it helps me, it gives me strength, it brings me mental clarity in order to be able to connect to God."], [38.1, "And if that is our aim and frame, that is service."], [42.4, "The important thing to ask ourselves, more even than what am I doing, is why am I doing it?"], [49.68, "And what is the highest and most valuable reason for doing so?"], [53.9, "When it is to serve and connect to our best selves and ultimately connect to God, that itself is."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-lATv7o1P000", "title": "Pay Attention to THIS | Thought For the Day #16", "short_title": "Pay Attention to THIS", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 16, "blurb": "In a world full of distractions, your attention is your most valuable currency. 📍 From the laws of making matzah to the words of the Shema, the Torah teaches us that connection starts not with love, but with listening.\n\nBefore we can love, we must pay attention. Who in your life needs your undivided attention today?", "date": "2026-02-05", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/042-lATv7o1P000.mp3", "duration": 58, "audio_local": "audio/042-lATv7o1P000.mp3", "transcript": "One of the most valuable and precious gifts that you can give anyone is your attention. And it's because of that that one of the first מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> that we have in the תורה <i>Torah</i> is making מצה <i>matzah</i>, interestingly. And the way to make מצה <i>matzah</i> is that you take wheat and water that can become חמץ <i>chametz</i>, it can become leavened and become completely prohibited on פסח <i>Pesach</i>. And the only way to ensure that it remains מצה <i>matzah</i> is to pay attention to it, to give your attention to it. And so God says the first act that you do is essentially to learn to pay attention because that attention will become essential in the relationship that we have with God. In the שמע ישראל <i>Shema Yisrael</i>, the first thing is not love, the first point is שמע <i>Shema</i>, listen. God before asking us to love asks for our attention. One of the greatest gifts that we can give is that. So today, if there's someone that you care about, give them a precious gift, your undivided attention.", "sentences": [[0.0, "One of the most valuable and precious gifts that you can give anyone is your attention."], [6.12, "And it's because of that that one of the first מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> that we have in the תורה <i>Torah</i> is making מצה <i>matzah</i>, interestingly."], [11.12, "And the way to make מצה <i>matzah</i> is that you take wheat and water that can become חמץ <i>chametz</i>, it can become leavened and become completely prohibited on פסח <i>Pesach</i>."], [20.52, "And the only way to ensure that it remains מצה <i>matzah</i> is to pay attention to it, to give your attention to it."], [26.2, "And so God says the first act that you do is essentially to learn to pay attention because that attention will become essential in the relationship that we have with God."], [37.72, "In the שמע ישראל <i>Shema Yisrael</i>, the first thing is not love, the first point is שמע <i>Shema</i>, listen."], [44.98, "God before asking us to love asks for our attention."], [49.08, "One of the greatest gifts that we can give is that."], [51.56, "So today, if there's someone that you care about, give them a precious gift, your undivided attention."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-ZnsE9oZlWMo", "title": "The Root of Antisemitism | Thought For the Day #15", "short_title": "The Root of Antisemitism", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 15, "blurb": "The Root of Antisemitism | Thought For the Day #15", "date": "2026-02-04", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/043-ZnsE9oZlWMo.mp3", "duration": 59, "audio_local": "audio/043-ZnsE9oZlWMo.mp3", "transcript": "The word antisemitism comes from anti-שם <i>Shem</i>, which literally means against name. And that means that the hatred that comes to the Jewish people has some element of a resentment, a hatred towards the Jewish people speaking their names proudly and boldly, essentially living their identity. Be proud that you're Jewish because the world is going to remind you anyway. The Jewish people live in pride and strength, and the stronger our Judaism, the stronger our Jewish identity, the stronger our Jewish values, the more we recognize the value of our names. But if we respond to antisemitism, the anti-name hatred, by reducing our names, we've already capitulated and we've given in. The best response to antisemitism is to turn the volume up on our name as Jews in the world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The word antisemitism comes from anti-שם <i>Shem</i>, which literally means against name."], [7.44, "And that means that the hatred that comes to the Jewish people has some element of a resentment, a hatred towards the Jewish people speaking their names proudly and boldly, essentially living their identity."], [24.52, "Be proud that you're Jewish because the world is going to remind you anyway."], [28.16, "The Jewish people live in pride and strength, and the stronger our Judaism, the stronger our Jewish identity, the stronger our Jewish values, the more we recognize the value of our names."], [40.8, "But if we respond to antisemitism, the anti-name hatred, by reducing our names, we've already capitulated and we've given in."], [49.68, "The best response to antisemitism is to turn the volume up on our name as Jews in the world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-2C542nMf_YI", "title": "The Dark Side of Charity | Thought For the Day #14", "short_title": "The Dark Side of Charity", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 14, "blurb": "The Dark Side of Charity | Thought For the Day #14", "date": "2026-02-03", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/044-2C542nMf_YI.mp3", "duration": 73, "audio_local": "audio/044-2C542nMf_YI.mp3", "transcript": "Sometimes giving can actually damage the person we're trying to help. צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>, charity, and חסד <i>Chesed</i>, kindness, are central to תורה <i>Torah</i> and Judaism. But our sages are very clear. How we give can lift a person or quietly break them. If we give and at the same time take away their dignity, we've missed the goal. When we give, we aren't just handing over money or help. We're also changing the balance in the human relationship. By definition, the recipient is put in a more vulnerable position. To a certain degree, the recipient is standing because as the giver we enabled it. If we aren't sensitive to that, our giving can create shame, dependence, or feeling of inadequacy. That's the opposite of what צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is meant to do. The better we are at preserving someone's self-respect, the higher the level of the מצווה <i>Mitzvah</i>. Real צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is generosity plus dignity, doing everything we can to help while keeping a person's independence and self-respect intact. Next time we give, whether it's money, time, or even a favor, we can pause and ask, is there a way to do this that protects their dignity even more? Sometimes that small adjustment makes a world of difference.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Sometimes giving can actually damage the person we're trying to help."], [4.64, "צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i>, charity, and חסד <i>Chesed</i>, kindness, are central to תורה <i>Torah</i> and Judaism."], [9.38, "But our sages are very clear."], [10.88, "How we give can lift a person or quietly break them."], [14.38, "If we give and at the same time take away their dignity, we've missed the goal."], [18.82, "When we give, we aren't just handing over money or help."], [22.14, "We're also changing the balance in the human relationship."], [25.38, "By definition, the recipient is put in a more vulnerable position."], [29.2, "To a certain degree, the recipient is standing because as the giver we enabled it."], [34.16, "If we aren't sensitive to that, our giving can create shame, dependence, or feeling of inadequacy."], [40.68, "That's the opposite of what צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is meant to do."], [44.08, "The better we are at preserving someone's self-respect, the higher the level of the מצווה <i>Mitzvah</i>."], [48.9, "Real צדקה <i>Tzedakah</i> is generosity plus dignity, doing everything we can to help while keeping a person's independence and self-respect intact."], [58.2, "Next time we give, whether it's money, time, or even a favor, we can pause and ask, is there a way to do this that protects their dignity even more?"], [67.3, "Sometimes that small adjustment makes a world of difference."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-mVVvyVBTcPI", "title": "How Do We Reach God? | Thought For the Day #13", "short_title": "How Do We Reach God?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 13, "blurb": "How Do We Reach God? | Thought For the Day #13", "date": "2026-02-02", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/045-mVVvyVBTcPI.mp3", "duration": 77, "audio_local": "audio/045-mVVvyVBTcPI.mp3", "transcript": "How are we supposed to connect to God? We say God isn't like us in any way, no body, no needs, no emotions like ours. That creates a problem. How do you have a relationship with a being completely unlike you? Well, we need to hear from him how it is that we're supposed to relate to him. That's what the commandments, the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are. Every מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> is an opportunity to say, \\\"I heard you. I care about what you care about. I'm here.\\\" In a human relationship we do little things like make coffee, a phone call, send a message, not because the person can't survive without these things, but because we love them. מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> work the same way, small acts of love, not items on a spreadsheet. The prophet Isaiah warns us about this very issue and says, בפיו ובשפתיו כבדוני ולבו רחק ממני <i>b'piv u-bi'sfatav kibduni v-libo rachak mimeni</i>, the nation draws near to me with its mouth, but its heart is far from me. That's מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> becoming automatic. The issue isn't that people aren't doing the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, they are. It's that they become disconnected from the relationship. The body is doing the action, but the heart's clocked out. So the question isn't \\\"do I do the מצוה <i>mitzvah</i>,\\\" the question is \\\"did I approach this מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> as an act of love?\\\" Every מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> is a way of showing our love to God in a way that lands with him.", "sentences": [[0.0, "How are we supposed to connect to God?"], [2.56, "We say God isn't like us in any way, no body, no needs, no emotions like ours."], [7.66, "That creates a problem."], [9.3, "How do you have a relationship with a being completely unlike you?"], [12.9, "Well, we need to hear from him how it is that we're supposed to relate to him."], [17.04, "That's what the commandments, the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> are."], [19.5, "Every מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> is an opportunity to say, \\\"I heard you."], [22.9, "I care about what you care about."], [24.82, "I'm here.\\\" In a human relationship we do little things like make coffee, a phone call, send a message, not because the person can't survive without these things, but because we love them."], [35.64, "מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> work the same way, small acts of love, not items on a spreadsheet."], [40.76, "The prophet Isaiah warns us about this very issue and says, בפיו ובשפתיו כבדוני ולבו רחק ממני <i>b'piv u-bi'sfatav kibduni v-libo rachak mimeni</i>, the nation draws near to me with its mouth, but its heart is far from me."], [47.9, "That's מצוות <i>mitzvot</i> becoming automatic."], [50.76, "The issue isn't that people aren't doing the מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, they are."], [53.28, "It's that they become disconnected from the relationship."], [54.34, "The body is doing the action, but the heart's clocked out."], [59.32, "So the question isn't \\\"do I do the מצוה <i>mitzvah</i>,\\\" the question is \\\"did I approach this מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> as an act of love?\\\" Every מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> is a way of showing our love to God in a way that lands with him."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-umXZBKC3Fao", "title": "Are You Sure? | Thought For the Day #12", "short_title": "Are You Sure?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 12, "blurb": "Are You Sure? | Thought For the Day #12", "date": "2026-02-01", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/046-umXZBKC3Fao.mp3", "duration": 92, "audio_local": "audio/046-umXZBKC3Fao.mp3", "transcript": "Just because we think doesn't mean that we think accurately. And that's something that we have a bit of a hard time with because whenever it is that we think of something or think about something, we assume that what we're thinking is correct. When more often than not, it isn't. Because our minds, our brains are not wired for truth. Our brains are wired for security and survival. And that means that we are biased towards security and survival even with regards to our own feelings of inadequacy or being wrong about something. Because to be wrong about something means that we're not quite in touch with reality. And so we will rationalize everything that we can without even thinking about it. Like, we don't actually sit and rationalize thoughtfully. Our brains are wired to do this. And so what תורה <i>Torah</i> says to us to do in order to be able to safeguard from this is first of all to be humble. And humility is not just so that we're better people, but so that we can get to truth easily, more accurately, because when we think of ourselves less, we open our minds to being wrong and we open our minds to changing what it is that we think. And that's really important and a very high value of the תורה <i>Torah</i>. The other thing that תורה <i>Torah</i> says is spend time with חכמים <i>chachamim</i>. Spend time with people who have trained for a long time to be able to calibrate their thinking accurately and truthfully. And when we spend time with these kinds of people, because we're social animals, we tend to learn to do things like them and we become more in line.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Just because we think doesn't mean that we think accurately."], [4.72, "And that's something that we have a bit of a hard time with because whenever it is that we think of something or think about something, we assume that what we're thinking is correct."], [14.08, "When more often than not, it isn't."], [16.94, "Because our minds, our brains are not wired for truth."], [20.14, "Our brains are wired for security and survival."], [23.32, "And that means that we are biased towards security and survival even with regards to our own feelings of inadequacy or being wrong about something."], [32.28, "Because to be wrong about something means that we're not quite in touch with reality."], [35.78, "And so we will rationalize everything that we can without even thinking about it."], [38.98, "Like, we don't actually sit and rationalize thoughtfully."], [41.84, "Our brains are wired to do this."], [44.32, "And so what תורה <i>Torah</i> says to us to do in order to be able to safeguard from this is first of all to be humble."], [50.5, "And humility is not just so that we're better people, but so that we can get to truth easily, more accurately, because when we think of ourselves less, we open our minds to being wrong and we open our minds to changing what it is that we think."], [65.76, "And that's really important and a very high value of the תורה <i>Torah</i>."], [69.2, "The other thing that תורה <i>Torah</i> says is spend time with חכמים <i>chachamim</i>."], [72.7, "Spend time with people who have trained for a long time to be able to calibrate their thinking accurately and truthfully."], [80.4, "And when we spend time with these kinds of people, because we're social animals, we tend to learn to do things like them and we become more in line."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-RoE3QmpJ2Yg", "title": "Why Do We Say So Many Blessings? | Thought For the Day #11", "short_title": "Why Do We Say So Many Blessings?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 11, "blurb": "Why Do We Say So Many Blessings? | Thought For the Day #11", "date": "2026-01-31", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/047-RoE3QmpJ2Yg.mp3", "duration": 81, "audio_local": "audio/047-RoE3QmpJ2Yg.mp3", "transcript": "Why did the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> want us making blessings all the time? In the תורה <i>Torah</i>, there's only one explicit command to make a blessing: ברכת המזון <i>Birkat Hamazon</i>, the blessing after a satisfying meal. Its purpose is clear there in the subsequent verse, so we don't forget God in our satiation and start thinking it was my strength, my success, my power that did this. From that one command, the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> built an entire world of ברכות <i>Brachot</i>: before we eat, when we smell something pleasant, when we see something beautiful. So that all through the day, we get these tiny alerts: stop, notice, this is part of God's world and he's sharing it with you. They're like little sparks in the middle of an ordinary day that say, you're not alone, this moment is connected to something bigger. Without them, we slide very easily into I worked, I earned, I built this life. ברכות <i>Brachot</i> gently cut through that and remind us, this is a gift. It can be said that God lacks nothing except the one thing that he does not take unless you give it to him: your consciousness. A ברכה <i>bracha</i> is you offering God your attention for a few seconds in the middle of your everyday life. That's the real offering. The חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> didn't create blessings to burden us, they created a network of consciousness points that we carry with us everywhere. Today, choose a ברכה <i>bracha</i>. Say it slowly, with full intent, and give God the gift of your attention.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Why did the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> want us making blessings all the time?"], [3.36, "In the תורה <i>Torah</i>, there's only one explicit command to make a blessing: ברכת המזון <i>Birkat Hamazon</i>, the blessing after a satisfying meal."], [9.6, "Its purpose is clear there in the subsequent verse, so we don't forget God in our satiation and start thinking it was my strength, my success, my power that did this."], [19.56, "From that one command, the חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> built an entire world of ברכות <i>Brachot</i>: before we eat, when we smell something pleasant, when we see something beautiful."], [26.76, "So that all through the day, we get these tiny alerts: stop, notice, this is part of God's world and he's sharing it with you."], [34.7, "They're like little sparks in the middle of an ordinary day that say, you're not alone, this moment is connected to something bigger."], [41.56, "Without them, we slide very easily into I worked, I earned, I built this life."], [47.28, "ברכות <i>Brachot</i> gently cut through that and remind us, this is a gift."], [51.06, "It can be said that God lacks nothing except the one thing that he does not take unless you give it to him: your consciousness."], [58.7, "A ברכה <i>bracha</i> is you offering God your attention for a few seconds in the middle of your everyday life."], [63.5, "That's the real offering."], [65.72, "The חכמים <i>Hachamim</i> didn't create blessings to burden us, they created a network of consciousness points that we carry with us everywhere."], [73.16, "Today, choose a ברכה <i>bracha</i>."], [75.52, "Say it slowly, with full intent, and give God the gift of your attention."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-NJ1NvA5tOYY", "title": "Where We Find Freedom | Thought For the Day #10", "short_title": "Where We Find Freedom", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 10, "blurb": "Where We Find Freedom | Thought For the Day #10", "date": "2026-01-29", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/048-NJ1NvA5tOYY.mp3", "duration": 73, "audio_local": "audio/048-NJ1NvA5tOYY.mp3", "transcript": "What if real freedom isn't I do whatever I want, but I choose who I will become? That counter-intuitively means exercising freedom in what you limit yourself to. We usually think of freedom as no limits, but Judaism has a different take. Freedom is the space where we can actually be who we really are, without the things that block us. Freedom is the ability to be all that we are, without the wrong things getting in the way. And some of those blocks are outside us, like circumstances, oppression, injustice, but some are inside: fear, compulsiveness, laziness, needing approval, or old stories that we tell ourselves. To really be free, we actually have to choose good limits. We limit our time on certain things so we can build something real. We limit our impulses so we can have deeper relationships. We limit certain options so we can fully commit to the life that matters to us. If we don't choose our limits, they choose us: our biology, our fears, social pressure. Freedom is being able to say, I will live inside this commitment, this value, this relationship, so that I can be fully myself. Ask yourself today, what am I willing to limit myself to, so that my best self can actually emerge?", "sentences": [[0.56, "What if real freedom isn't I do whatever I want, but I choose who I will become?"], [5.92, "That counter-intuitively means exercising freedom in what you limit yourself to."], [10.32, "We usually think of freedom as no limits, but Judaism has a different take."], [15.06, "Freedom is the space where we can actually be who we really are, without the things that block us."], [20.6, "Freedom is the ability to be all that we are, without the wrong things getting in the way."], [25.42, "And some of those blocks are outside us, like circumstances, oppression, injustice, but some are inside: fear, compulsiveness, laziness, needing approval, or old stories that we tell ourselves."], [38.14, "To really be free, we actually have to choose good limits."], [41.26, "We limit our time on certain things so we can build something real."], [44.38, "We limit our impulses so we can have deeper relationships."], [47.3, "We limit certain options so we can fully commit to the life that matters to us."], [51.68, "If we don't choose our limits, they choose us: our biology, our fears, social pressure."], [57.58, "Freedom is being able to say, I will live inside this commitment, this value, this relationship, so that I can be fully myself."], [64.98, "Ask yourself today, what am I willing to limit myself to, so that my best self can actually emerge?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-CoF44g1Pl0s", "title": "When Do You Stop? | Thought For the Day #9", "short_title": "When Do You Stop?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 9, "blurb": "When Do You Stop? | Thought For the Day #9", "date": "2026-01-28", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/049-CoF44g1Pl0s.mp3", "duration": 72, "audio_local": "audio/049-CoF44g1Pl0s.mp3", "transcript": "We sometimes have to stop and ask ourselves, are we human beings or just human doings? In the תורה <i>Torah</i>, שבת <i>Shabbat</i> isn't just a day off. It's a day when we stop creating. We stop making more things, more output, more projects, so we can be conscious of what's happening with what we've done. Most of the week we live as human doings: emails, meetings, errands, projects, constant producing. שבת <i>Shabbat</i> is a day of rest from creative work, from the kind of work where we're bringing something new into existence that wasn't there before. Creating is beautiful, but if we never stop, we never see the product of our lives. It just becomes an endless process. If we're always in motion, we never actually meet what we've created. There's no moment of, \\\"This is what I've built, this is my week, this is what I've become.\\\" שבת <i>Shabbat</i> is that pause. Once a week we stop producing so that what we've already done can just be for a while. Resting on שבת <i>Shabbat</i> isn't an escape from life; it's what lets our lives exist without us constantly fixing them, improving them, or adding to them. The תורה <i>Torah</i> tells us: once a week, just be. Nothing more needs to be done right now. And when we let ourselves be, we're closer to...", "sentences": [[0.55, "We sometimes have to stop and ask ourselves, are we human beings or just human doings?"], [5.92, "In the תורה <i>Torah</i>, שבת <i>Shabbat</i> isn't just a day off."], [8.3, "It's a day when we stop creating."], [10.36, "We stop making more things, more output, more projects, so we can be conscious of what's happening with what we've done."], [16.32, "Most of the week we live as human doings: emails, meetings, errands, projects, constant producing."], [23.36, "שבת <i>Shabbat</i> is a day of rest from creative work, from the kind of work where we're bringing something new into existence that wasn't there before."], [30.9, "Creating is beautiful, but if we never stop, we never see the product of our lives."], [35.84, "It just becomes an endless process."], [38.24, "If we're always in motion, we never actually meet what we've created."], [42.26, "There's no moment of, \\\"This is what I've built, this is my week, this is what I've become.\\\" שבת <i>Shabbat</i> is that pause."], [49.44, "Once a week we stop producing so that what we've already done can just be for a while."], [54.14, "Resting on שבת <i>Shabbat</i> isn't an escape from life; it's what lets our lives exist without us constantly fixing them, improving them, or adding to them."], [62.28, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> tells us: once a week, just be."], [66.02, "Nothing more needs to be done right now."], [68.26, "And when we let ourselves be, we're closer to..."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-iclMdsR-TRU", "title": "Do You Read Tehillim? | Thought For the Day #8", "short_title": "Do You Read Tehillim?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 8, "blurb": "Do You Read Tehillim? | Thought For the Day #8", "date": "2026-01-27", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/050-iclMdsR-TRU.mp3", "duration": 93, "audio_local": "audio/050-iclMdsR-TRU.mp3", "transcript": "The Book of תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>, the Book of Psalms, that we traditionally believe to be written and edited by דוד המלך <i>David Hamelech</i>, King David, is one of the most beautiful books of the canon of the Bible, of the תנ״ך <i>Tanakh</i>. It is filled with soaring praise of God in every way, for every situation. And when a person actually is able to speak the תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>, to read the תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> before God with an awareness of what it is that one is saying, that is one of the most beautiful endeavors that a person can do together with God. And when people treat תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> instead as a magic spellbook or an incantation book in which if I read these words in the right way, or if I read them this many times, or if I read them backwards and forwards, things will happen for me, I will get God to do things for me, is essentially antithetical to what תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is meant to be. To read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is to lean closer to God, to bring your consciousness, His engagement in your life. And they are written by the King of Israel and הרמב״ם <i>HaRambam</i> beautifully says, לב מלך, לב כל ישראל הוא <i>lev melech, lev kol Yisrael hu</i>. The heart of a king is the heart of all of Israel. And תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> for us is a national treasure. When we read it with understanding, with care, it is one of the greatest things that we can do. But when we read it as an attempt to manipulate things into happening, takes away and almost is against everything that תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is meant to be.", "sentences": [[0.6, "The Book of תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>, the Book of Psalms, that we traditionally believe to be written and edited by דוד המלך <i>David Hamelech</i>, King David, is one of the most beautiful books of the canon of the Bible, of the תנ״ך <i>Tanakh</i>."], [13.8, "It is filled with soaring praise of God in every way, for every situation."], [19.94, "And when a person actually is able to speak the תהילים <i>Tehillim</i>, to read the תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> before God with an awareness of what it is that one is saying, that is one of the most beautiful endeavors that a person can do together with God."], [33.64, "And when people treat תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> instead as a magic spellbook or an incantation book in which if I read these words in the right way, or if I read them this many times, or if I read them backwards and forwards, things will happen for me, I will get God to do things for me, is essentially antithetical to what תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is meant to be."], [53.86, "To read תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is to lean closer to God, to bring your consciousness, His engagement in your life."], [62.02, "And they are written by the King of Israel and הרמב״ם <i>HaRambam</i> beautifully says, לב מלך, לב כל ישראל הוא <i>lev melech, lev kol Yisrael hu</i>."], [66.1, "The heart of a king is the heart of all of Israel."], [68.22, "And תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> for us is a national treasure."], [69.3, "When we read it with understanding, with care, it is one of the greatest things that we can do."], [76.4, "But when we read it as an attempt to manipulate things into happening, takes away and almost is against everything that תהילים <i>Tehillim</i> is meant to be."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-iCRqwBk0Vik", "title": "Love God but Reject Science? Think Again | Thought For the Day #7", "short_title": "Love God but Reject Science? Think Again", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 7, "blurb": "Love God but Reject Science? Think Again | Thought For the Day #7", "date": "2026-01-26", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/051-iCRqwBk0Vik.mp3", "duration": 89, "audio_local": "audio/051-iCRqwBk0Vik.mp3", "transcript": "You can't love God and reject science. It's a big problem because God is the one who made the world and science is the study of the natural world. And although people will say yes, it's empirical, empirical meaning that the only way that we know about science is our observations of it and how much can we rely on our human observations? There's a point with that. But remember that science is not just about observations and theory. We build technologies based on our theory. We predict futures based on our understanding of the nature of reality, the natural world. And that means that to whatever degree we can as human beings, we're understanding the world that God created. We're coming to know it and have a genuine interaction with it that is real and not just a figment of our own imaginations. And that means that we come to know the work of the creator more accurately. And if you love the creator, of course it goes without saying that you love the creator's creations. It would be like saying to somebody I love you but I really could care less about anything that you do. If you love God, you love science. You love his world. You do what you can in order to be able to understand it better. You put your heart into understanding it because it's not just an academic endeavor when it comes to God, it's an act of love to come to know him and understand him.", "sentences": [[0.56, "You can't love God and reject science."], [3.76, "It's a big problem because God is the one who made the world and science is the study of the natural world."], [12.26, "And although people will say yes, it's empirical, empirical meaning that the only way that we know about science is our observations of it and how much can we rely on our human observations?"], [23.34, "There's a point with that."], [24.82, "But remember that science is not just about observations and theory."], [28.1, "We build technologies based on our theory."], [30.62, "We predict futures based on our understanding of the nature of reality, the natural world."], [37.34, "And that means that to whatever degree we can as human beings, we're understanding the world that God created."], [43.7, "We're coming to know it and have a genuine interaction with it that is real and not just a figment of our own imaginations."], [51.68, "And that means that we come to know the work of the creator more accurately."], [56.78, "And if you love the creator, of course it goes without saying that you love the creator's creations."], [63.2, "It would be like saying to somebody I love you but I really could care less about anything that you do."], [67.94, "If you love God, you love science."], [70.6, "You love his world."], [71.54, "You do what you can in order to be able to understand it better."], [75.76, "You put your heart into understanding it because it's not just an academic endeavor when it comes to God, it's an act of love to come to know him and understand him."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-S1qgjXqzDtQ", "title": "When You Don't Feel Love... | Thought For the Day #6", "short_title": "When You Don't Feel Love...", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 6, "blurb": "When You Don't Feel Love... | Thought For the Day #6", "date": "2026-01-25", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/052-S1qgjXqzDtQ.mp3", "duration": 89, "audio_local": "audio/052-S1qgjXqzDtQ.mp3", "transcript": "If love is only an emotion, what happens to our relationships on the days we don't feel it? In Western culture, we usually treat love as an emotion, something we feel. In תורה <i>Torah</i>, love, אהבה <i>ahavah</i>, isn't primarily a feeling. It's action, a way we live and act towards someone we've brought into our circle of care. For תורה <i>Torah</i>, love is what we do for someone's good and welfare. How we speak about them, how we protect them, how we show up for them. The רמב״ם <i>Rambam</i> says the מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> ואהבת לרעך כמוך <i>ve’ahavta l’re’acha kamocha</i>, to love your fellow as yourself, means we speak in praise of our fellow, we care for their money, their honor like our own. And we never build ourselves up by putting them down. We do that whether we feel affection towards the person or not. To love God, too, is not primarily to feel something inside. It's to act for Him, to study His תורה <i>Torah</i>, keep His מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, shape our lives around what He values. Feelings come and go. Some days we feel warm, inspired and close. Other days we feel flat or even annoyed. If love is only a feeling, then our love disappears every time the feeling dips. But if love is a process, how we speak, how we give, how we show loyalty, then we can keep loving even when the feelings are tired or complicated. True love shows up most clearly in how we treat someone when the feelings aren't easy. Today we can ask: what is one loving thing I could do for this person's good? That's where real love lives.", "sentences": [[0.0, "If love is only an emotion, what happens to our relationships on the days we don't feel it?"], [5.1, "In Western culture, we usually treat love as an emotion, something we feel."], [9.84, "In תורה <i>Torah</i>, love, אהבה <i>ahavah</i>, isn't primarily a feeling."], [14.3, "It's action, a way we live and act towards someone we've brought into our circle of care."], [19.14, "For תורה <i>Torah</i>, love is what we do for someone's good and welfare."], [22.52, "How we speak about them, how we protect them, how we show up for them."], [26.12, "The רמב״ם <i>Rambam</i> says the מצוה <i>mitzvah</i> ואהבת לרעך כמוך <i>ve’ahavta l’re’acha kamocha</i>, to love your fellow as yourself, means we speak in praise of our fellow, we care for their money, their honor like our own."], [36.96, "And we never build ourselves up by putting them down."], [40.54, "We do that whether we feel affection towards the person or not."], [44.16, "To love God, too, is not primarily to feel something inside."], [48.24, "It's to act for Him, to study His תורה <i>Torah</i>, keep His מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, shape our lives around what He values."], [54.3, "Feelings come and go."], [55.88, "Some days we feel warm, inspired and close."], [58.78, "Other days we feel flat or even annoyed."], [61.0, "If love is only a feeling, then our love disappears every time the feeling dips."], [65.74, "But if love is a process, how we speak, how we give, how we show loyalty, then we can keep loving even when the feelings are tired or complicated."], [75.2, "True love shows up most clearly in how we treat someone when the feelings aren't easy."], [80.48, "Today we can ask: what is one loving thing I could do for this person's good?"], [86.0, "That's where real love lives."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-tt7jEKOzNb4", "title": "Do You Kiss the Mezuza? | Thought For the Day #5", "short_title": "Do You Kiss the Mezuza?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 5, "blurb": "Do You Kiss the Mezuza? | Thought For the Day #5", "date": "2026-01-22", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/053-tt7jEKOzNb4.mp3", "duration": 71, "audio_local": "audio/053-tt7jEKOzNb4.mp3", "transcript": "הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> teaches us that the מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> is not a magic amulet. Now, the truth is we didn't need הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> to tell us that because the תורה <i>Torah</i> doesn't present the מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> as a magic amulet. It simply says you put these things on your doorposts to remember my מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, to remember my word, to remember me. And הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> at the end of הלכות מזוזה <i>hilchot mezuzah</i> says that the point of מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> is that when you pass through the door, if you're going out into the world, you pause for a minute and you remember the unity of God. That there is no other source for anything in this world except him. And that he is at the roots of it all and he protects you. There is no other source of protection. And that when you come in from the chaos of the world back into your home, you pause for a minute at the entrance. And it's a stimulus. The מזוזה <i>mezuzah's</i> a stimulus to remember God, his unity, and his מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>. And that when we stop there and don't simply mindlessly kiss it or consider it to be some kind of magic amulet, we actually spend time re-engaging with God in the midst of our daily life and activities.", "sentences": [[0.0, "הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> teaches us that the מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> is not a magic amulet."], [6.2, "Now, the truth is we didn't need הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> to tell us that because the תורה <i>Torah</i> doesn't present the מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> as a magic amulet."], [11.96, "It simply says you put these things on your doorposts to remember my מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>, to remember my word, to remember me."], [19.8, "And הרמב\\\"ם <i>Harambam</i> at the end of הלכות מזוזה <i>hilchot mezuzah</i> says that the point of מזוזה <i>mezuzah</i> is that when you pass through the door, if you're going out into the world, you pause for a minute and you remember the unity of God."], [31.52, "That there is no other source for anything in this world except him."], [35.14, "And that he is at the roots of it all and he protects you."], [39.68, "There is no other source of protection."], [42.78, "And that when you come in from the chaos of the world back into your home, you pause for a minute at the entrance."], [48.22, "And it's a stimulus."], [48.98, "The מזוזה <i>mezuzah's</i> a stimulus to remember God, his unity, and his מצוות <i>mitzvot</i>."], [55.14, "And that when we stop there and don't simply mindlessly kiss it or consider it to be some kind of magic amulet, we actually spend time re-engaging with God in the midst of our daily life and activities."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-p_Ggw-oeKK0", "title": "You Are a Work of Art | Thought For the Day #4", "short_title": "You Are a Work of Art", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 4, "blurb": "You Are a Work of Art | Thought For the Day #4", "date": "2026-01-21", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/054-p_Ggw-oeKK0.mp3", "duration": 62, "audio_local": "audio/054-p_Ggw-oeKK0.mp3", "transcript": "Why does a great song, book, or work of art sometimes feel almost sacred to us? Judaism teaches that God is the only source for everything that exists. And that means that all that we see and experience in the world is, in some way, His expression, His work. We can come to know an artist when we experience their art. And in a similar way, we can come to know God by giving our attention to His portfolio, which is the world itself. That includes not just mountains, oceans, and galaxies, but people and their creations. Because our creativity is a part of His creation. So when a song, a piece of art, or poem really reaches us, we're touching something of the divine in existence, because through their work, God's creativity and reality are shining through. Next time a song or a painting or even a view stops us in our tracks, we can pause for a minute and think, this too is part of how God is speaking in the world.", "sentences": [[0.0, "Why does a great song, book, or work of art sometimes feel almost sacred to us?"], [6.36, "Judaism teaches that God is the only source for everything that exists."], [9.86, "And that means that all that we see and experience in the world is, in some way, His expression, His work."], [18.26, "We can come to know an artist when we experience their art."], [21.78, "And in a similar way, we can come to know God by giving our attention to His portfolio, which is the world itself."], [29.21, "That includes not just mountains, oceans, and galaxies, but people and their creations."], [34.76, "Because our creativity is a part of His creation."], [37.66, "So when a song, a piece of art, or poem really reaches us, we're touching something of the divine in existence, because through their work, God's creativity and reality are shining through."], [50.42, "Next time a song or a painting or even a view stops us in our tracks, we can pause for a minute and think, this too is part of how God is speaking in the world."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-doxOVjKLNhs", "title": "Who Are You Really? | Thought For the Day #3", "short_title": "Who Are You Really?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 3, "blurb": "Who Are You Really? | Thought For the Day #3", "date": "2026-01-20", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/055-doxOVjKLNhs.mp3", "duration": 93, "audio_local": "audio/055-doxOVjKLNhs.mp3", "transcript": "If we never leave what's familiar, we never find out who we really are. God's first words to Abraham are לך לך <i>Lech Lecha</i>. Go for yourself. Leave your land, your birthplace, your father's house. God's telling him, if you're going to become who you really are, you can't just stay inside what's familiar. Each of us have a completely individual existence, a unique expression of God that will never appear in the world again in exactly the same way. But as long as we stand only inside the same stories, the same expectations, the same roles we grew up with, parts of us never get a chance to emerge and become integrated into a whole identity. Abraham has to step away from his given context in order for him to see who he is both for himself and before God. Leaving home isn't only physical. It can mean stepping out of the family script, questioning the assumptions we were handed, or putting ourselves into new and different contexts. When we stand in front of alternative backdrops, different people, cultures, responsibilities, new latent aspects of ourselves start to show. We discover strengths, values, and flaws we never knew we had, because they were never tested. That doesn't mean we never go back home. It means that every time we do return, we come back a little closer to the person we were born to be. We can ask ourselves, where am I stubbornly keeping to only the familiar? And what is one step I can take, one new context, one new experience, to let more of my true self emerge?", "sentences": [[0.0, "If we never leave what's familiar, we never find out who we really are."], [4.68, "God's first words to Abraham are לך לך <i>Lech Lecha</i>."], [7.2, "Go for yourself."], [8.52, "Leave your land, your birthplace, your father's house."], [11.42, "God's telling him, if you're going to become who you really are, you can't just stay inside what's familiar."], [17.4, "Each of us have a completely individual existence, a unique expression of God that will never appear in the world again in exactly the same way."], [25.3, "But as long as we stand only inside the same stories, the same expectations, the same roles we grew up with, parts of us never get a chance to emerge and become integrated into a whole identity."], [37.22, "Abraham has to step away from his given context in order for him to see who he is both for himself and before God."], [44.62, "Leaving home isn't only physical."], [46.72, "It can mean stepping out of the family script, questioning the assumptions we were handed, or putting ourselves into new and different contexts."], [54.76, "When we stand in front of alternative backdrops, different people, cultures, responsibilities, new latent aspects of ourselves start to show."], [64.3, "We discover strengths, values, and flaws we never knew we had, because they were never tested."], [70.06, "That doesn't mean we never go back home."], [72.28, "It means that every time we do return, we come back a little closer to the person we were born to be."], [78.32, "We can ask ourselves, where am I stubbornly keeping to only the familiar?"], [83.06, "And what is one step I can take, one new context, one new experience, to let more of my true self emerge?"]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-5kiIgI3HuQI", "title": "How Do You Talk to Yourself? | Thought For the Day #2", "short_title": "How Do You Talk to Yourself?", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 2, "blurb": "The way we speak to ourselves, matters.", "date": "2026-01-19", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/056-5kiIgI3HuQI.mp3", "duration": 88, "audio_local": "audio/056-5kiIgI3HuQI.mp3", "transcript": "The way that we talk to ourselves in our minds in our hearts matters. The תורה <i>Torah</i> of course teaches us to be kind to other people, to be caring with other people, to care about their feelings and to empathize with them. But we sometimes forget that that includes us. Right? It's not just others that we're supposed to be caring and kind to, it's ourselves. And we know that from the פסוק <i>pasuk</i>. פסוק <i>Pasuk</i> says ואהבת לרעך כמוך <i>ve'ahavta lere'acha kamocha</i>, you shall love others as yourself. And that of course means that the self is the referent, it is the point of reference to how one is meant to love. If we are meant to love others, we are certainly meant to love ourselves and to treat ourselves well. And there's a difference between that and selfishness. Selfishness is all I think about is me and I could care less about what happens around me. But self-care is just a genuine sensitivity to my own well-being. Because as הלל <i>Hillel</i> says אם אין אני לי מי לי <i>im ein ani li mi li</i>, if I'm not going to care for myself, well who is? There's nobody else that can live my life for me and there's nobody else that is truly going to care and make sure that I am well. And when I am well, I can make sure that others are well. How it is that we speak to ourselves, how we talk to ourselves internally, should be kind, should be gentle. It doesn't mean that we have to let ourselves off the hook and that we shouldn't be concerned about bettering ourselves, to the contrary, absolutely need to do that, but we can do that with compassion and kindness to ourselves as well.", "sentences": [[0.64, "The way that we talk to ourselves in our minds in our hearts matters."], [6.2, "The תורה <i>Torah</i> of course teaches us to be kind to other people, to be caring with other people, to care about their feelings and to empathize with them."], [13.98, "But we sometimes forget that that includes us."], [16.2, "Right?"], [16.76, "It's not just others that we're supposed to be caring and kind to, it's ourselves."], [20.18, "And we know that from the פסוק <i>pasuk</i>."], [22.12, "פסוק <i>Pasuk</i> says ואהבת לרעך כמוך <i>ve'ahavta lere'acha kamocha</i>, you shall love others as yourself."], [26.26, "And that of course means that the self is the referent, it is the point of reference to how one is meant to love."], [32.68, "If we are meant to love others, we are certainly meant to love ourselves and to treat ourselves well."], [38.2, "And there's a difference between that and selfishness."], [40.14, "Selfishness is all I think about is me and I could care less about what happens around me."], [45.24, "But self-care is just a genuine sensitivity to my own well-being."], [51.56, "Because as הלל <i>Hillel</i> says אם אין אני לי מי לי <i>im ein ani li mi li</i>, if I'm not going to care for myself, well who is?"], [56.26, "There's nobody else that can live my life for me and there's nobody else that is truly going to care and make sure that I am well."], [62.26, "And when I am well, I can make sure that others are well."], [64.54, "How it is that we speak to ourselves, how we talk to ourselves internally, should be kind, should be gentle."], [70.78, "It doesn't mean that we have to let ourselves off the hook and that we shouldn't be concerned about bettering ourselves, to the contrary, absolutely need to do that, but we can do that with compassion and kindness to ourselves as well."]]}, {"guid": "rabbi-dweck-tftd-nRenpUlwr7Q", "title": "The World is Getting Better! | Thought For the Day #1", "short_title": "The World is Getting Better!", "series": "Thought For the Day", "number": 1, "blurb": "Even with all the mess, it is getting better and better....", "date": "2026-01-18", "audio_url": "https://pub-070b719e63724e0aa2b8b4a7f824a153.r2.dev/audio/057-nRenpUlwr7Q.mp3", "duration": 78, "audio_local": "audio/057-nRenpUlwr7Q.mp3", "transcript": "The world is getting better. I know that that's shocking to a lot of people because of all the terrible stuff that's going on in the world at the moment. But if you think about it, terrible stuff has been going on in the world at almost every moment. And nonetheless, society and human experience on the world gets better. All you have to do is pan out and look at it in broad terms and recognize that hunger in the world is better, poverty in the world is better, general health in the world is better, life expectancy is tremendously higher than it used to be. So that is something to keep in mind when we look at the world and think that everything is a disaster and to remember that you can test it this way: which of us would prefer to move back to a hundred years ago and stay there? I don't mean to visit, stay there. There may be a few of you that would be interested in that, but I would be willing to bet that the great majority of us aren't interested in doing that, that we'd rather have all of the issues that we've got and still stay here in this world with everything that's going on. And on a תורה <i>Torah</i> level what that means is that God is moving his world into a better place, and although the process is sometimes less than pretty, it is nonetheless getting there. So we maintain hope.", "sentences": [[0.0, "The world is getting better."], [1.54, "I know that that's shocking to a lot of people because of all the terrible stuff that's going on in the world at the moment."], [8.48, "But if you think about it, terrible stuff has been going on in the world at almost every moment."], [13.88, "And nonetheless, society and human experience on the world gets better."], [19.66, "All you have to do is pan out and look at it in broad terms and recognize that hunger in the world is better, poverty in the world is better, general health in the world is better, life expectancy is tremendously higher than it used to be."], [33.72, "So that is something to keep in mind when we look at the world and think that everything is a disaster and to remember that you can test it this way: which of us would prefer to move back to a hundred years ago and stay there?"], [49.1, "I don't mean to visit, stay there."], [51.1, "There may be a few of you that would be interested in that, but I would be willing to bet that the great majority of us aren't interested in doing that, that we'd rather have all of the issues that we've got and still stay here in this world with everything that's going on."], [64.12, "And on a תורה <i>Torah</i> level what that means is that God is moving his world into a better place, and although the process is sometimes less than pretty, it is nonetheless getting there."], [75.12, "So we maintain hope."]]}];
